{"data":[{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04630","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04630","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04630","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vrushank","familyName":"Ahire","name":"Ahire, Vrushank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aniruddh","familyName":"Muley","name":"Muley, Aniruddh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shivam","familyName":"Zample","name":"Zample, Shivam","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Siddharth","familyName":"Verma","name":"Verma, Siddharth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pranav","familyName":"Menon","name":"Menon, Pranav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Surbhi","familyName":"Madan","name":"Madan, Surbhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Abhinav","familyName":"Dhall","name":"Dhall, Abhinav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SFANet: Spatial-Frequency Attention Network for Deepfake Detection"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:35:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:22:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Detecting manipulated media has now become a pressing issue with the recent rise of deepfakes. Most existing approaches fail to generalize across diverse datasets and generation techniques. We thus propose a novel ensemble framework, combining the strengths of transformer-based architectures, such as Swin Transformers and ViTs, and texture-based methods, to achieve better detection accuracy and robustness. Our method introduces innovative data-splitting, sequential training, frequency splitting, patch-based attention, and face segmentation techniques to handle dataset imbalances, enhance high-impact regions (e.g., eyes and mouth), and improve generalization. Our model achieves state-of-the-art performance when tested on the DFWild-Cup dataset, a diverse subset of eight deepfake datasets. The ensemble benefits from the complementarity of these approaches, with transformers excelling in global feature extraction and texturebased methods providing interpretability. This work demonstrates that hybrid models can effectively address the evolving challenges of deepfake detection, offering a robust solution for real-world applications."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04630","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:10Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:19:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04623","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04623","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04623","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shrish Shrinath","familyName":"Vaidya","name":"Vaidya, Shrish Shrinath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gowthamaan","familyName":"Palani","name":"Palani, Gowthamaan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sidharth","familyName":"Ramesh","name":"Ramesh, Sidharth","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Velmurugan","familyName":"Balasubramanian","name":"Balasubramanian, Velmurugan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minmini","familyName":"Selvam","name":"Selvam, Minmini","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gokulraja","familyName":"Srinivasaraja","name":"Srinivasaraja, Gokulraja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ganapathy","familyName":"Krishnamurthi","name":"Krishnamurthi, Ganapathy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MedPAO: A Protocol-Driven Agent for Structuring Medical Reports"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:32:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:22:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/978-3-032-06004-4_4","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The deployment of Large Language Models (LLMs) for structuring clinical data is critically hindered by their tendency to hallucinate facts and their inability to follow domain-specific rules. To address this, we introduce MedPAO, a novel agentic framework that ensures accuracy and verifiable reasoning by grounding its operation in established clinical protocols such as the ABCDEF protocol for CXR analysis. MedPAO decomposes the report structuring task into a transparent process managed by a Plan-Act-Observe (PAO) loop and specialized tools. This protocol-driven method provides a verifiable alternative to opaque, monolithic models. The efficacy of our approach is demonstrated through rigorous evaluation: MedPAO achieves an F1-score of 0.96 on the critical sub-task of concept categorization. Notably, expert radiologists and clinicians rated the final structured outputs with an average score of 4.52 out of 5, indicating a level of reliability that surpasses baseline approaches relying solely on LLM-based foundation models. The code is available at: https://github.com/MiRL-IITM/medpao-agent"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Paper published at \"Agentic AI for Medicine\" Workshop, MICCAI 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04623","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:19:00Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:19:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:19:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04605","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04605","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04605","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingyao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jingyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tianlin","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Tianlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaoyu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xiaoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring the Power of Diffusion Large Language Models for Software Engineering: An Empirical Investigation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T09:13:25Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:21:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Autoregressive Large Language Models (AR-LLMs) are widely used in software engineering (SE) but face limitations in processing code structure information and suffer from high inference latency. Diffusion LLMs (DLLMs) offer a promising alternative with global bidirectional encoding and decoupled generation steps. This work presents the first comprehensive evaluation of DLLMs across the software development lifecycle, including code generation, defect detection, and program repair. On a large-scale benchmark of 52,937 tasks, 7Bparameter DLLMs outperform AR-LLMs with a 30% average accuracy improvement achieving a 113% gain on cross-file repair, while maintaining superior efficiency and reduced latency. Our results establish DLLMs as a superior paradigm for SE tasks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04605","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:35Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:18:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04581","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04581","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04581","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dang","familyName":"Anh","name":"Anh, Dang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rick","familyName":"Nouwen","name":"Nouwen, Rick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Massimo","familyName":"Poesio","name":"Poesio, Massimo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Can LLMs Detect Ambiguous Plural Reference? An Analysis of Split-Antecedent and Mereological Reference"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:32:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Our goal is to study how LLMs represent and interpret plural reference in ambiguous and unambiguous contexts. We ask the following research questions: (1) Do LLMs exhibit human-like preferences in representing plural reference? (2) Are LLMs able to detect ambiguity in plural anaphoric expressions and identify possible referents? To address these questions, we design a set of experiments, examining pronoun production using next-token prediction tasks, pronoun interpretation, and ambiguity detection using different prompting strategies. We then assess how comparable LLMs are to humans in formulating and interpreting plural reference. We find that LLMs are sometimes aware of possible referents of ambiguous pronouns. However, they do not always follow human reference when choosing between interpretations, especially when the possible interpretation is not explicitly mentioned. In addition, they struggle to identify ambiguity without direct instruction. Our findings also reveal inconsistencies in the results across different types of experiments."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04581","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:18:00Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:18:01Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:18:01Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04577","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04577","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04577","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juncheng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Juncheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chao","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Chao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cheng","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Cheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoyu","familyName":"Xie","name":"Xie, Haoyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guoqi","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Guoqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lei","familyName":"Shang","name":"Shang, Lei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shujun","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Language Model Based Text-to-Audio Generation: Anti-Causally Aligned Collaborative Residual Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:26:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:32Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While language models (LMs) paired with residual vector quantization (RVQ) tokenizers have shown promise in text-to-audio (T2A) generation, they still lag behind diffusion-based models by a non-trivial margin. We identify a critical dilemma underpinning this gap: incorporating more RVQ layers improves audio reconstruction fidelity but exceeds the generation capacity of conventional LMs. To address this, we first analyze RVQ dynamics and uncover two key limitations: 1) orthogonality of features across RVQ layers hinders effective LMs training, and 2) descending semantic richness in tokens from deeper RVQ layers exacerbates exposure bias during autoregressive decoding. Based on these insights, we propose Siren, a novel LM-based framework that employs multiple isolated transformers with causal conditioning and anti-causal alignment via reinforcement learning. Extensive experiments demonstrate that Siren outperforms both existing LM-based and diffusion-based T2A systems, achieving state-of-the-art results. By bridging the representational strengths of LMs with the fidelity demands of audio synthesis, our approach repositions LMs as competitive contenders against diffusion models in T2A tasks. Moreover, by aligning audio representations with linguistic structures, Siren facilitates a promising pathway toward unified multi-modal generation frameworks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04577","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:54Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:17:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04568","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04568","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04568","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Naman","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Naman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shreeyash","familyName":"Gowaikar","name":"Gowaikar, Shreeyash","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arun","familyName":"Iyer","name":"Iyer, Arun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kirankumar","familyName":"Shiragur","name":"Shiragur, Kirankumar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ramakrishna B","familyName":"Bairi","name":"Bairi, Ramakrishna B","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishikesh","familyName":"Maurya","name":"Maurya, Rishikesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ritabrata","familyName":"Maiti","name":"Maiti, Ritabrata","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sankarshan","familyName":"Damle","name":"Damle, Sankarshan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shachee Mishra","familyName":"Gupta","name":"Gupta, Shachee Mishra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"COSMIR: Chain Orchestrated Structured Memory for Iterative Reasoning over Long Context"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T08:10:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:19:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning over very long inputs remains difficult for large language models (LLMs). Common workarounds either shrink the input via retrieval (risking missed evidence), enlarge the context window (straining selectivity), or stage multiple agents to read in pieces. In staged pipelines (e.g., Chain of Agents, CoA), free-form summaries passed between agents can discard crucial details and amplify early mistakes. We introduce COSMIR (Chain Orchestrated Structured Memory for Iterative Reasoning), a chain-style framework that replaces ad hoc messages with a structured memory. A Planner agent first turns a user query into concrete, checkable sub-questions. worker agents process chunks via a fixed micro-cycle: Extract, Infer, Refine, writing all updates to the shared memory. A Manager agent then Synthesizes the final answer directly from the memory. This preserves step-wise read-then-reason benefits while changing both the communication medium (structured memory) and the worker procedure (fixed micro-cycle), yielding higher faithfulness, better long-range aggregation, and auditability. On long-context QA from the HELMET suite, COSMIR reduces propagation-stage information loss and improves accuracy over a CoA baseline."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04568","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:41Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:17:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04560","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04560","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04560","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honghao","familyName":"Fu","name":"Fu, Honghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuan","familyName":"Ouyang","name":"Ouyang, Yuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kai-Wei","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Kai-Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiwei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yiwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zi","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yujun","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Yujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ContextNav: Towards Agentic Multimodal In-Context Learning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T07:49:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:18:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances demonstrate that multimodal large language models (MLLMs) exhibit strong multimodal in-context learning (ICL) capabilities, enabling them to adapt to novel vision-language tasks from a few contextual examples. However, existing ICL approaches face challenges in reconciling scalability with robustness across diverse tasks and noisy contextual examples: manually selecting examples produces clean contexts but is labor-intensive and task-specific, while similarity-based retrieval improves scalability but could introduce irrelevant or structurally inconsistent samples that degrade ICL performance. To address these limitations, we propose ContextNav, the first agentic framework that integrates the scalability of automated retrieval with the quality and adaptiveness of human-like curation, enabling noise-robust and dynamically optimized contextualization for multimodal ICL. ContextNav unifies context management and noise-robust contextualization within a closed-loop workflow driven by graph-based orchestration. Specifically, it builds a resource-aware multimodal embedding pipeline, maintains a retrievable vector database, and applies agentic retrieval and structural alignment to construct noise-resilient contexts. An Operational Grammar Graph (OGG) further supports adaptive workflow planning and optimization, enabling the agent to refine its operational strategies based on downstream ICL feedback. Experimental results demonstrate that ContextNav achieves state-of-the-art performance across various datasets, underscoring the promise of agentic workflows for advancing scalable and robust contextualization in multimodal ICL."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04560","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:17:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04542","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04542","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04542","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wolfgang","familyName":"Lehrach","name":"Lehrach, Wolfgang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Hennes","name":"Hennes, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Miguel","familyName":"Lazaro-Gredilla","name":"Lazaro-Gredilla, Miguel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinghua","familyName":"Lou","name":"Lou, Xinghua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Carter","familyName":"Wendelken","name":"Wendelken, Carter","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zun","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Antoine","familyName":"Dedieu","name":"Dedieu, Antoine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jordi","familyName":"Grau-Moya","name":"Grau-Moya, Jordi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc","familyName":"Lanctot","name":"Lanctot, Marc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Atil","familyName":"Iscen","name":"Iscen, Atil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"John","familyName":"Schultz","name":"Schultz, John","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marcus","familyName":"Chiam","name":"Chiam, Marcus","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ian","familyName":"Gemp","name":"Gemp, Ian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Piotr","familyName":"Zielinski","name":"Zielinski, Piotr","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satinder","familyName":"Singh","name":"Singh, Satinder","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kevin P.","familyName":"Murphy","name":"Murphy, Kevin P.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Code World Models for General Game Playing"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T07:16:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:17:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) reasoning abilities are increasingly being applied to classical board and card games, but the dominant approach -- involving prompting for direct move generation -- has significant drawbacks. It relies on the model's implicit fragile pattern-matching capabilities, leading to frequent illegal moves and strategically shallow play. Here we introduce an alternative approach: We use the LLM to translate natural language rules and game trajectories into a formal, executable world model represented as Python code. This generated model -- comprising functions for state transition, legal move enumeration, and termination checks -- serves as a verifiable simulation engine for high-performance planning algorithms like Monte Carlo tree search (MCTS). In addition, we prompt the LLM to generate heuristic value functions (to make MCTS more efficient), and inference functions (to estimate hidden states in imperfect information games). Our method offers three distinct advantages compared to directly using the LLM as a policy: (1) Verifiability: The generated CWM serves as a formal specification of the game's rules, allowing planners to algorithmically enumerate valid actions and avoid illegal moves, contingent on the correctness of the synthesized model; (2) Strategic Depth: We combine LLM semantic understanding with the deep search power of classical planners; and (3) Generalization: We direct the LLM to focus on the meta-task of data-to-code translation, enabling it to adapt to new games more easily. We evaluate our agent on 10 different games, of which 4 are novel and created for this paper. 5 of the games are fully observed (perfect information), and 5 are partially observed (imperfect information). We find that our method outperforms or matches Gemini 2.5 Pro in 9 out of the 10 considered games."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04542","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:17:03Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:17:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:17:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04536","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04536","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04536","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shun-ichiro","familyName":"Hayashi","name":"Hayashi, Shun-ichiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daichi","familyName":"Mukunoki","name":"Mukunoki, Daichi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tetsuya","familyName":"Hoshino","name":"Hoshino, Tetsuya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Satoshi","familyName":"Ohshima","name":"Ohshima, Satoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takahiro","familyName":"Katagiri","name":"Katagiri, Takahiro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"3Dify: a Framework for Procedural 3D-CG Generation Assisted by LLMs Using MCP and RAG"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Graphics (cs.GR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T07:00:06Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:17:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper proposes \"3Dify,\" a procedural 3D computer graphics (3D-CG) generation framework utilizing Large Language Models (LLMs). The framework enables users to generate 3D-CG content solely through natural language instructions. 3Dify is built upon Dify, an open-source platform for AI application development, and incorporates several state-of-the-art LLM-related technologies such as the Model Context Protocol (MCP) and Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG). For 3D-CG generation support, 3Dify automates the operation of various Digital Content Creation (DCC) tools via MCP. When DCC tools do not support MCP-based interaction, the framework employs the Computer-Using Agent (CUA) method to automate Graphical User Interface (GUI) operations. Moreover, to enhance image generation quality, 3Dify allows users to provide feedback by selecting preferred images from multiple candidates. The LLM then learns variable patterns from these selections and applies them to subsequent generations. Furthermore, 3Dify supports the integration of locally deployed LLMs, enabling users to utilize custom-developed models and to reduce both time and monetary costs associated with external API calls by leveraging their own computational resources."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04536","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:55Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:16:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04528","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04528","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04528","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Santhosh","familyName":"KumarRavindran","name":"KumarRavindran, Santhosh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Unified Threat Detection and Mitigation Framework (UTDMF): Combating Prompt Injection, Deception, and Bias in Enterprise-Scale Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T06:44:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:16:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rapid adoption of large language models (LLMs) in enterprise systems exposes vulnerabilities to prompt injection attacks, strategic deception, and biased outputs, threatening security, trust, and fairness. Extending our adversarial activation patching framework (arXiv:2507.09406), which induced deception in toy networks at a 23.9% rate, we introduce the Unified Threat Detection and Mitigation Framework (UTDMF), a scalable, real-time pipeline for enterprise-grade models like Llama-3.1 (405B), GPT-4o, and Claude-3.5. Through 700+ experiments per model, UTDMF achieves: (1) 92% detection accuracy for prompt injection (e.g., jailbreaking); (2) 65% reduction in deceptive outputs via enhanced patching; and (3) 78% improvement in fairness metrics (e.g., demographic bias). Novel contributions include a generalized patching algorithm for multi-threat detection, three groundbreaking hypotheses on threat interactions (e.g., threat chaining in enterprise workflows), and a deployment-ready toolkit with APIs for enterprise integration."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04528","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:42Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:43Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:16:43Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04519","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04519","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04519","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heiko","familyName":"Koziolek","name":"Koziolek, Heiko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thilo","familyName":"Braun","name":"Braun, Thilo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Virendra","familyName":"Ashiwal","name":"Ashiwal, Virendra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sofia","familyName":"Linsbauer","name":"Linsbauer, Sofia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marthe Ahlgreen","familyName":"Hansen","name":"Hansen, Marthe Ahlgreen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karoline","familyName":"Grotterud","name":"Grotterud, Karoline","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spec2Control: Automating PLC/DCS Control-Logic Engineering from Natural Language Requirements with LLMs - A Multi-Plant Evaluation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T06:21:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:16:30Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Distributed control systems (DCS) manage the automation for many industrial production processes (e.g., power plants, chemical refineries, steel mills). Programming the software for such systems remains a largely manual and tedious process, incurring costs of millions of dollars for extensive facilities. Large language models (LLMs) have been found helpful in generating DCS control logic, resulting in commercial copilot tools. Today, these tools are focused on textual notations, they provide limited automation, and have not been tested on large datasets with realistic test cases. We introduce Spec2Control, a highly automated LLM workflow to generate graphical control logic directly from natural language user requirements. Experiments using an open dataset with 10 control narratives and 65 complex test cases demonstrate that Spec2Control can successfully identify control strategies, can generate 98.6% of correct control strategy connections autonomously, and can save between 94-96% of human labor. Spec2Control is being integrated into commercial ABB engineering tools, but is also available as an open-source variant for independent validation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"12 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04519","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:29Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:30Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:16:30Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04506","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04506","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04506","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiashuo","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Jiashuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shixuan","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Shixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaochen","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Zhaochen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xianrui","familyName":"Zhong","name":"Zhong, Xianrui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pengcheng","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Pengcheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiran","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Peiran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Weijia","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Weijia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiawei","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Jiawei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GRACE: Generative Representation Learning via Contrastive Policy Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T05:46:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:15:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Prevailing methods for training Large Language Models (LLMs) as text encoders rely on contrastive losses that treat the model as a black box function, discarding its generative and reasoning capabilities in favor of static embeddings. We introduce GRACE (Generative Representation Learning via Contrastive Policy Optimization), a novel framework that reimagines contrastive signals not as losses to be minimized, but as rewards that guide a generative policy. In GRACE, the LLM acts as a policy that produces explicit, human-interpretable rationales--structured natural language explanations of its semantic understanding. These rationales are then encoded into high-quality embeddings via mean pooling. Using policy gradient optimization, we train the model with a multi-component reward function that maximizes similarity between query positive pairs and minimizes similarity with negatives. This transforms the LLM from an opaque encoder into an interpretable agent whose reasoning process is transparent and inspectable. On MTEB benchmark, GRACE yields broad cross category gains: averaged over four backbones, the supervised setting improves overall score by 11.5% over base models, and the unsupervised variant adds 6.9%, while preserving general capabilities. This work treats contrastive objectives as rewards over rationales, unifying representation learning with generation to produce stronger embeddings and transparent rationales. The model, data and code are available at https://github.com/GasolSun36/GRACE."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"23 pages, 7 figures, 7 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04506","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:16:11Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:16:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:16:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04498","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04498","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04498","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Qiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Adnan","familyName":"Labib","name":"Labib, Adnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Swier","name":"Swier, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Michael","familyName":"Hofmeyr","name":"Hofmeyr, Michael","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zheng","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Zheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GenQuest: An LLM-based Text Adventure Game for Language Learners"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T05:22:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:15:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"GenQuest is a generative text adventure game that leverages Large Language Models (LLMs) to facilitate second language learning through immersive, interactive storytelling. The system engages English as a Foreign Language (EFL) learners in a collaborative \"choose-your-own-adventure\" style narrative, dynamically generated in response to learner choices. Game mechanics such as branching decision points and story milestones are incorporated to maintain narrative coherence while allowing learner-driven plot development. Key pedagogical features include content generation tailored to each learner's proficiency level, and a vocabulary assistant that provides in-context explanations of learner-queried text strings, ranging from words and phrases to sentences. Findings from a pilot study with university EFL students in China indicate promising vocabulary gains and positive user perceptions. Also discussed are suggestions from participants regarding the narrative length and quality, and the request for multi-modal content such as illustrations."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Workshop on Wordplay: When Language Meets Games, EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04498","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:59Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:15:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04493","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04493","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04493","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinyan","familyName":"Su","name":"Su, Jinyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Claire","familyName":"Cardie","name":"Cardie, Claire","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jennifer","familyName":"Healey","name":"Healey, Jennifer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Hop Question Answering: When Can Humans Help, and Where do They Struggle?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T05:07:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:14:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-hop question answering is a challenging task for both large language models (LLMs) and humans, as it requires recognizing when multi-hop reasoning is needed, followed by reading comprehension, logical reasoning, and knowledge integration. To better understand how humans might collaborate effectively with AI, we evaluate the performance of crowd workers on these individual reasoning subtasks. We find that while humans excel at knowledge integration (97\\% accuracy), they often fail to recognize when a question requires multi-hop reasoning (67\\% accuracy). Participants perform reasonably well on both single-hop and multi-hop QA (84\\% and 80\\% accuracy, respectively), but frequently make semantic mistakes--for example, answering \"when\" an event happened when the question asked \"where.\" These findings highlight the importance of designing AI systems that complement human strengths while compensating for common weaknesses."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04493","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:51Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:52Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:15:52Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04488","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04488","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04488","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edward Y.","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Edward Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ethan Y.","familyName":"Chang","name":"Chang, Ethan Y.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Agent Collaborative Intelligence: Dual-Dial Control for Reliable LLM Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Theory (cs.IT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.4","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T04:52:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:14:36Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-agent debate often wastes compute by using a fixed adversarial stance, aggregating without deliberation, or stopping on heuristics. We introduce MACI, an active controller with two independent dials that decouple information from behavior: an information dial that gates evidence by quality, and a behavior dial that schedules contentiousness from exploration to consolidation. A moderator tracks disagreement, overlap, evidence quality, and argument quality, and halts when gains plateau. We provide theory-lite guarantees for nonincreasing dispersion and provable termination, with a budget-feasible scheduler. Across clinical diagnosis and news-bias tasks, MACI improves accuracy and calibration while reducing tokens, and converts residual uncertainty into precision RAG plans that specify what to retrieve next. We use a cross-family LLM judge (CRIT) as a conservative soft weight and stop signal, validated for order invariance and judge-swap stability; stability depends on using high-capability judges. MACI turns debate into a budget-aware, measurable, and provably terminating controller."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 5 figures, 21 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04488","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:15:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04477","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04477","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04477","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Soo Yong","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Soo Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Suin","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Suin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vincent-Daniel","familyName":"Yun","name":"Yun, Vincent-Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Gyeongyeon","familyName":"Hwang","name":"Hwang, Gyeongyeon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MedCLM: Learning to Localize and Reason via a CoT-Curriculum in Medical Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T04:26:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:13:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Bridging clinical diagnostic reasoning with AI remains a central challenge in medical imaging. We introduce MedCLM, an automated pipeline that converts detection datasets into large-scale medical visual question answering (VQA) data with Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning by linking lesion boxes to organ segmentation and structured rationales. These contextual signals enable medical vision-language models to generate question-answer pairs with step-by-step reasoning. To utilize this data effectively, we propose an Integrated CoT-Curriculum Strategy composed of an Easy stage with explicit lesion boxes for visual grounding, a Medium stage that encourages implicit localization, and a Hard stage for weakly supervised reasoning. Experimental results demonstrate that MedCLM attains state-of-the-art performance on several medical VQA benchmarks, providing a scalable framework for developing clinically aligned medical vision-language models."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04477","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:29Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:15:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04463","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04463","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04463","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Takashi","familyName":"Maekaku","name":"Maekaku, Takashi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keita","familyName":"Goto","name":"Goto, Keita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinchuan","familyName":"Tian","name":"Tian, Jinchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yusuke","familyName":"Shinohara","name":"Shinohara, Yusuke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shinji","familyName":"Watanabe","name":"Watanabe, Shinji","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluating Self-Supervised Speech Models via Text-Based LLMS"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T03:25:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:13:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Self-Supervised Learning (SSL) has gained traction for its ability to learn rich representations with low labeling costs, applicable across diverse downstream tasks. However, assessing the downstream-task performance remains challenging due to the cost of extra training and evaluation. Existing methods for task-agnostic evaluation also require extra training or hyperparameter tuning. We propose a novel evaluation metric using large language models (LLMs). By inputting discrete token sequences and minimal domain cues derived from SSL models into LLMs, we obtain the mean log-likelihood; these cues guide in-context learning, rendering the score more reliable without extra training or hyperparameter tuning. Experimental results show a correlation between LLM-based scores and automatic speech recognition task. Additionally, our findings reveal that LLMs not only functions as an SSL evaluation tools but also provides inference-time embeddings that are useful for speaker verification task."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to ASRU 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04463","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:15:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:15:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:15:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04454","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04454","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04454","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangchi","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Xiangchi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Xiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tong","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Tong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dachuan","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Dachuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Can","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Can","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenke","familyName":"Lee","name":"Lee, Wenke","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saayan","familyName":"Mitra","name":"Mitra, Saayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mitigating Forgetting Between Supervised and Reinforcement Learning Yields Stronger Reasoners"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T03:01:14Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:12:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) show strong reasoning abilities, often amplified by Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting and reinforcement learning (RL). Although RL algorithms can substantially improve reasoning, they struggle to expand reasoning boundaries because they learn from their own reasoning trajectories rather than acquiring external knowledge. Supervised fine-tuning (SFT) offers complementary benefits but typically requires large-scale data and risks overfitting. Recent attempts to combine SFT and RL face three main challenges: data inefficiency, algorithm-specific designs, and catastrophic forgetting. We propose a plug-and-play framework that dynamically integrates SFT into RL by selecting challenging examples for SFT. This approach reduces SFT data requirements and remains agnostic to the choice of RL or SFT algorithm. To mitigate catastrophic forgetting of RL-acquired skills during SFT, we select high-entropy tokens for loss calculation and freeze parameters identified as critical for RL. Our method achieves state-of-the-art (SoTA) reasoning performance using only 1.5% of the SFT data and 20.4% of the RL data used by prior SoTA, providing an efficient and plug-and-play solution for combining SFT and RL in reasoning post-training."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04454","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:14:54Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:14:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:14:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04450","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04450","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04450","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiyuan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Qiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yicong","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yicong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haotian","familyName":"Ye","name":"Ye, Haotian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinghao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Jinghao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinyao","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Xinyao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pheng-Ann","familyName":"Heng","name":"Heng, Pheng-Ann","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefano","familyName":"Ermon","name":"Ermon, Stefano","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Angela","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Angela","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"REAR: Rethinking Visual Autoregressive Models via Generator-Tokenizer Consistency Regularization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T02:48:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:12:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Visual autoregressive (AR) generation offers a promising path toward unifying vision and language models, yet its performance remains suboptimal against diffusion models. Prior work often attributes this gap to tokenizer limitations and rasterization ordering. In this work, we identify a core bottleneck from the perspective of generator-tokenizer inconsistency, i.e., the AR-generated tokens may not be well-decoded by the tokenizer. To address this, we propose reAR, a simple training strategy introducing a token-wise regularization objective: when predicting the next token, the causal transformer is also trained to recover the visual embedding of the current token and predict the embedding of the target token under a noisy context. It requires no changes to the tokenizer, generation order, inference pipeline, or external models. Despite its simplicity, reAR substantially improves performance. On ImageNet, it reduces gFID from 3.02 to 1.86 and improves IS to 316.9 using a standard rasterization-based tokenizer. When applied to advanced tokenizers, it achieves a gFID of 1.42 with only 177M parameters, matching the performance with larger state-of-the-art diffusion models (675M)."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"27 pages, 23 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04450","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:14:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:14:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:14:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04439","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04439","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04439","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lucie","familyName":"Kunitomo-Jacquin","name":"Kunitomo-Jacquin, Lucie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Edison","familyName":"Marrese-Taylor","name":"Marrese-Taylor, Edison","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ken","familyName":"Fukuda","name":"Fukuda, Ken","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Role of Unobserved Sequences on Sample-based Uncertainty Quantification for LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T02:14:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:11:16Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Quantifying uncertainty in large language models (LLMs) is important for safety-critical applications because it helps spot incorrect answers, known as hallucinations. One major trend of uncertainty quantification methods is based on estimating the entropy of the distribution of the LLM's potential output sequences. This estimation is based on a set of output sequences and associated probabilities obtained by querying the LLM several times. In this paper, we advocate and experimentally show that the probability of unobserved sequences plays a crucial role, and we recommend future research to integrate it to enhance such LLM uncertainty quantification methods."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to UncertaiNLP workshop of EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04439","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:14:33Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:14:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:14:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04401","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04401","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04401","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuyang","familyName":"Guo","name":"Guo, Xuyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zekai","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zekai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenmei","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Zhenmei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhao","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Zhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiahao","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Jiahao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Your Vision-Language Model Can't Even Count to 20: Exposing the Failures of VLMs in Compositional Counting"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T00:11:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:09:10Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Vision-Language Models (VLMs) have become a central focus of today's AI community, owing to their impressive abilities gained from training on large-scale vision-language data from the Web. These models have demonstrated strong performance across diverse tasks, including image understanding, video understanding, complex visual reasoning, and embodied AI. Despite these noteworthy successes, a fundamental question remains: Can VLMs count objects correctly? In this paper, we introduce a simple yet effective benchmark, VLMCountBench, designed under a minimalist setting with only basic geometric shapes (e.g., triangles, circles) and their compositions, focusing exclusively on counting tasks without interference from other factors. We adopt strict independent variable control and systematically study the effects of simple properties such as color, size, and prompt refinement in a controlled ablation. Our empirical results reveal that while VLMs can count reliably when only one shape type is present, they exhibit substantial failures when multiple shape types are combined (i.e., compositional counting). This highlights a fundamental empirical limitation of current VLMs and motivates important directions for future research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04401","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:13:36Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:13:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:13:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04400","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04400","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04400","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marc","familyName":"Cavazza","name":"Cavazza, Marc","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Large Language Models Preserve Semantic Isotopies in Story Continuations"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-06T00:03:12Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:09:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In this work, we explore the relevance of textual semantics to Large Language Models (LLMs), extending previous insights into the connection between distributional semantics and structural semantics. We investigate whether LLM-generated texts preserve semantic isotopies. We design a story continuation experiment using 10,000 ROCStories prompts completed by five LLMs. We first validate GPT-4o's ability to extract isotopies from a linguistic benchmark, then apply it to the generated stories. We then analyze structural (coverage, density, spread) and semantic properties of isotopies to assess how they are affected by completion. Results show that LLM completion within a given token horizon preserves semantic isotopies across multiple properties."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04400","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:13:35Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:13:35Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:13:35Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04392","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04392","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04392","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Faisal","familyName":"Hamman","name":"Hamman, Faisal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenyang","familyName":"Zhu","name":"Zhu, Chenyang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anoop","familyName":"Kumar","name":"Kumar, Anoop","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xujun","familyName":"Peng","name":"Peng, Xujun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sanghamitra","familyName":"Dutta","name":"Dutta, Sanghamitra","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daben","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Daben","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alfy","familyName":"Samuel","name":"Samuel, Alfy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Improving Consistency in Retrieval-Augmented Systems with Group Similarity Rewards"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T23:14:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:08:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"RAG systems are increasingly deployed in high-stakes domains where users expect outputs to be consistent across semantically equivalent queries. However, existing systems often exhibit significant inconsistencies due to variability in both the retriever and generator (LLM), undermining trust and reliability. In this work, we focus on information consistency, i.e., the requirement that outputs convey the same core content across semantically equivalent inputs. We introduce a principled evaluation framework that decomposes RAG consistency into retriever-level, generator-level, and end-to-end components, helping identify inconsistency sources. To improve consistency, we propose Paraphrased Set Group Relative Policy Optimization (PS-GRPO), an RL approach that leverages multiple rollouts across paraphrased set to assign group similarity rewards. We leverage PS-GRPO to achieve Information Consistent RAG (Con-RAG), training the generator to produce consistent outputs across paraphrased queries and remain robust to retrieval-induced variability. Because exact reward computation over paraphrase sets is computationally expensive, we also introduce a scalable approximation method that retains effectiveness while enabling efficient, large-scale training. Empirical evaluations across short-form, multi-hop, and long-form QA benchmarks demonstrate that Con-RAG significantly improves both consistency and accuracy over strong baselines, even in the absence of explicit ground-truth supervision. Our work provides practical solutions for evaluating and building reliable RAG systems for safety-critical deployments."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on Reliable ML from Unreliable Data"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04392","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:13:23Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:13:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:13:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04386","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04386","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04386","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shakson","familyName":"Isaac","name":"Isaac, Shakson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yentl","familyName":"Collin","name":"Collin, Yentl","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chirag","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Chirag","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SSM-CGM: Interpretable State-Space Forecasting Model of Continuous Glucose Monitoring for Personalized Diabetes Management"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T22:37:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:08:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Continuous glucose monitoring (CGM) generates dense data streams critical for diabetes management, but most used forecasting models lack interpretability for clinical use. We present SSM-CGM, a Mamba-based neural state-space forecasting model that integrates CGM and wearable activity signals from the AI-READI cohort. SSM-CGM improves short-term accuracy over a Temporal Fusion Transformer baseline, adds interpretability through variable selection and temporal attribution, and enables counterfactual forecasts simulating how planned changes in physiological signals (e.g., heart rate, respiration) affect near-term glucose. Together, these features make SSM-CGM an interpretable, physiologically grounded framework for personalized diabetes management."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Shakson Isaac and Yentl Collin contributed equally"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04386","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:13:14Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:13:15Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:13:15Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04364","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04364","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04364","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Morita","familyName":"Tarvirdians","name":"Tarvirdians, Morita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Senthil","familyName":"Chandrasegaran","name":"Chandrasegaran, Senthil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hayley","familyName":"Hung","name":"Hung, Hayley","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Catholijn M.","familyName":"Jonker","name":"Jonker, Catholijn M.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Catharine","familyName":"Oertel","name":"Oertel, Catharine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Reflection Before Action: Designing a Framework for Quantifying Thought Patterns for Increased Self-awareness in Personal Decision Making"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T21:16:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:06:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"When making significant life decisions, people increasingly turn to conversational AI tools, such as large language models (LLMs). However, LLMs often steer users toward solutions, limiting metacognitive awareness of their own decision-making. In this paper, we shift the focus in decision support from solution-orientation to reflective activity, coining the term pre-decision reflection (PDR). We introduce PROBE, the first framework that assesses pre-decision reflections along two dimensions: breadth (diversity of thought categories) and depth (elaborateness of reasoning). Coder agreement demonstrates PROBE's reliability in capturing how people engage in pre-decision reflection. Our study reveals substantial heterogeneity across participants and shows that people perceived their unassisted reflections as deeper and broader than PROBE's measures. By surfacing hidden thought patterns, PROBE opens opportunities for technologies that foster self-awareness and strengthen people's agency in choosing which thought patterns to rely on in decision-making."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04364","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:42Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:12:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04357","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04357","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04357","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anoushka","familyName":"Harit","name":"Harit, Anoushka","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhongtian","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Zhongtian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jongmin","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Jongmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From News to Returns: A Granger-Causal Hypergraph Transformer on the Sphere"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computational Finance (q-fin.CP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Economics and business","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T20:51:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:06:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We propose the Causal Sphere Hypergraph Transformer (CSHT), a novel architecture for interpretable financial time-series forecasting that unifies \\emph{Granger-causal hypergraph structure}, \\emph{Riemannian geometry}, and \\emph{causally masked Transformer attention}. CSHT models the directional influence of financial news and sentiment on asset returns by extracting multivariate Granger-causal dependencies, which are encoded as directional hyperedges on the surface of a hypersphere. Attention is constrained via angular masks that preserve both temporal directionality and geometric consistency. Evaluated on S\\\u0026amp;P 500 data from 2018 to 2023, including the 2020 COVID-19 shock, CSHT consistently outperforms baselines across return prediction, regime classification, and top-asset ranking tasks. By enforcing predictive causal structure and embedding variables in a Riemannian manifold, CSHT delivers both \\emph{robust generalisation across market regimes} and \\emph{transparent attribution pathways} from macroeconomic events to stock-level responses. These results suggest that CSHT is a principled and practical solution for trustworthy financial forecasting under uncertainty."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"6th ACM International Conference on AI in Finance"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04357","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:32Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:12:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04342","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04342","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04342","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshil","familyName":"Vejendla","name":"Vejendla, Harshil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Learning to Predict Chaos: Curriculum-Driven Training for Robust Forecasting of Chaotic Dynamics"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T20:06:16Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:05:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Forecasting chaotic systems is a cornerstone challenge in many scientific fields, complicated by the exponential amplification of even infinitesimal prediction errors. Modern machine learning approaches often falter due to two opposing pitfalls: over-specializing on a single, well-known chaotic system (e.g., Lorenz-63), which limits generalizability, or indiscriminately mixing vast, unrelated time-series, which prevents the model from learning the nuances of any specific dynamical regime. We propose Curriculum Chaos Forecasting (CCF), a training paradigm that bridges this gap. CCF organizes training data based on fundamental principles of dynamical systems theory, creating a curriculum that progresses from simple, periodic behaviors to highly complex, chaotic dynamics. We quantify complexity using the largest Lyapunov exponent and attractor dimension, two well-established metrics of chaos. By first training a sequence model on predictable systems and gradually introducing more chaotic trajectories, CCF enables the model to build a robust and generalizable representation of dynamical behaviors. We curate a library of over 50 synthetic ODE/PDE systems to build this curriculum. Our experiments show that pre-training with CCF significantly enhances performance on unseen, real-world benchmarks. On datasets including Sunspot numbers, electricity demand, and human ECG signals, CCF extends the valid prediction horizon by up to 40% compared to random-order training and more than doubles it compared to training on real-world data alone. We demonstrate that this benefit is consistent across various neural architectures (GRU, Transformer) and provide extensive ablations to validate the importance of the curriculum's structure."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"MIT URTC Technical Paper (Oral), 5 pages, 4 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04342","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:09Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:10Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:12:10Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04341","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04341","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04341","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"G. Niklas","familyName":"Noren","name":"Noren, G. Niklas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eva-Lisa","familyName":"Meldau","name":"Meldau, Eva-Lisa","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Johan","familyName":"Ellenius","name":"Ellenius, Johan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Critical appraisal of artificial intelligence for rare-event recognition: principles and pharmacovigilance case studies"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.0","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T20:05:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:05:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Many high-stakes AI applications target low-prevalence events, where apparent accuracy can conceal limited real-world value. Relevant AI models range from expert-defined rules and traditional machine learning to generative LLMs constrained for classification. We outline key considerations for critical appraisal of AI in rare-event recognition, including problem framing and test set design, prevalence-aware statistical evaluation, robustness assessment, and integration into human workflows. In addition, we propose an approach to structured case-level examination (SCLE), to complement statistical performance evaluation, and a comprehensive checklist to guide procurement or development of AI models for rare-event recognition. We instantiate the framework in pharmacovigilance, drawing on three studies: rule-based retrieval of pregnancy-related reports; duplicate detection combining machine learning with probabilistic record linkage; and automated redaction of person names using an LLM. We highlight pitfalls specific to the rare-event setting including optimism from unrealistic class balance and lack of difficult positive controls in test sets - and show how cost-sensitive targets align model performance with operational value. While grounded in pharmacovigilance practice, the principles generalize to domains where positives are scarce and error costs may be asymmetric."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04341","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:12:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04339","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04339","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04339","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Christian","familyName":"Limberg","name":"Limberg, Christian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fares","familyName":"Schulz","name":"Schulz, Fares","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhe","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Zhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stefan","familyName":"Weinzierl","name":"Weinzierl, Stefan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Pitch-Conditioned Instrument Sound Synthesis From an Interactive Timbre Latent Space"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T20:03:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:05:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper presents a novel approach to neural instrument sound synthesis using a two-stage semi-supervised learning framework capable of generating pitch-accurate, high-quality music samples from an expressive timbre latent space. Existing approaches that achieve sufficient quality for music production often rely on high-dimensional latent representations that are difficult to navigate and provide unintuitive user experiences. We address this limitation through a two-stage training paradigm: first, we train a pitch-timbre disentangled 2D representation of audio samples using a Variational Autoencoder; second, we use this representation as conditioning input for a Transformer-based generative model. The learned 2D latent space serves as an intuitive interface for navigating and exploring the sound landscape. We demonstrate that the proposed method effectively learns a disentangled timbre space, enabling expressive and controllable audio generation with reliable pitch conditioning. Experimental results show the model's ability to capture subtle variations in timbre while maintaining a high degree of pitch accuracy. The usability of our method is demonstrated in an interactive web application, highlighting its potential as a step towards future music production environments that are both intuitive and creatively empowering: https://pgesam.faresschulz.com"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, accepted to the Proceedings of the 28-th Int. Conf. on Digital Audio Effects (DAFx25) - demo: https://pgesam.faresschulz.com"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04339","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:05Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:12:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04338","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04338","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04338","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mathieu","familyName":"Laï-king","name":"Laï-king, Mathieu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Patrick","familyName":"Paroubek","name":"Paroubek, Patrick","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Evaluation of Clinical Trials Reporting Quality using Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T20:01:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:05:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reporting quality is an important topic in clinical trial research articles, as it can impact clinical decisions. In this article, we test the ability of large language models to assess the reporting quality of this type of article using the Consolidated Standards of Reporting Trials (CONSORT). We create CONSORT-QA, an evaluation corpus from two studies on abstract reporting quality with CONSORT-abstract standards. We then evaluate the ability of different large generative language models (from the general domain or adapted to the biomedical domain) to correctly assess CONSORT criteria with different known prompting methods, including Chain-of-thought. Our best combination of model and prompting method achieves 85% accuracy. Using Chain-of-thought adds valuable information on the model's reasoning for completing the task."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04338","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:12:04Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:12:04Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:12:04Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04320","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04320","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04320","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yihao","familyName":"Quan","name":"Quan, Yihao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zeru","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Zeru","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenting","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zhenting","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanshu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yanshu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruixiang","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Ruixiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Read the Scene, Not the Script: Outcome-Aware Safety for LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T18:46:49Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:04:33Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Safety-aligned Large Language Models (LLMs) still show two dominant failure modes: they are easily jailbroken, or they over-refuse harmless inputs that contain sensitive surface signals. We trace both to a common cause: current models reason weakly about links between actions and outcomes and over-rely on surface-form signals, lexical or stylistic cues that do not encode consequences. We define this failure mode as Consequence-blindness. To study consequence-blindness, we build a benchmark named CB-Bench covering four risk scenarios that vary whether semantic risk aligns with outcome risk, enabling evaluation under both matched and mismatched conditions which are often ignored by existing safety benchmarks. Mainstream models consistently fail to separate these risks and exhibit consequence-blindness, indicating that consequence-blindness is widespread and systematic. To mitigate consequence-blindness, we introduce CS-Chain-4k, a consequence-reasoning dataset for safety alignment. Models fine-tuned on CS-Chain-4k show clear gains against semantic-camouflage jailbreaks and reduce over-refusal on harmless inputs, while maintaining utility and generalization on other benchmarks. These results clarify the limits of current alignment, establish consequence-aware reasoning as a core alignment goal and provide a more practical and reproducible evaluation path."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04320","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:11:37Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:11:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:11:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04317","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04317","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04317","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yucong","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Yucong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mashrur","familyName":"Chowdhury","name":"Chowdhury, Mashrur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yongkai","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Yongkai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FairAgent: Democratizing Fairness-Aware Machine Learning with LLM-Powered Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T18:33:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:04:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Training fair and unbiased machine learning models is crucial for high-stakes applications, yet it presents significant challenges. Effective bias mitigation requires deep expertise in fairness definitions, metrics, data preprocessing, and machine learning techniques. In addition, the complex process of balancing model performance with fairness requirements while properly handling sensitive attributes makes fairness-aware model development inaccessible to many practitioners. To address these challenges, we introduce FairAgent, an LLM-powered automated system that significantly simplifies fairness-aware model development. FairAgent eliminates the need for deep technical expertise by automatically analyzing datasets for potential biases, handling data preprocessing and feature engineering, and implementing appropriate bias mitigation strategies based on user requirements. Our experiments demonstrate that FairAgent achieves significant performance improvements while significantly reducing development time and expertise requirements, making fairness-aware machine learning more accessible to practitioners."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by ICDM 2025 Demo Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04317","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:11:33Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:11:33Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:11:33Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04311","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04311","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04311","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohan","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Bohan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huidong","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Huidong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Keyue","familyName":"Jiang","name":"Jiang, Keyue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaowen","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Xiaowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Importance of Task Complexity in Evaluating LLM-Based Multi-Agent Systems"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T18:08:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:03:41Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language model multi-agent systems (LLM-MAS) offer a promising paradigm for harnessing collective intelligence to achieve more advanced forms of AI behaviour. While recent studies suggest that LLM-MAS can outperform LLM single-agent systems (LLM-SAS) on certain tasks, the lack of systematic experimental designs limits the strength and generality of these conclusions. We argue that a principled understanding of task complexity, such as the degree of sequential reasoning required and the breadth of capabilities involved, is essential for assessing the effectiveness of LLM-MAS in task solving. To this end, we propose a theoretical framework characterising tasks along two dimensions: depth, representing reasoning length, and width, representing capability diversity. We theoretically examine a representative class of LLM-MAS, namely the multi-agent debate system, and empirically evaluate its performance in both discriminative and generative tasks with varying depth and width. Theoretical and empirical results show that the benefit of LLM-MAS over LLM-SAS increases with both task depth and width, and the effect is more pronounced with respect to depth. This clarifies when LLM-MAS are beneficial and provides a principled foundation for designing future LLM-MAS methods and benchmarks."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04311","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:11:24Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:11:25Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:11:25Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04304","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04304","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04304","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshil","familyName":"Vejendla","name":"Vejendla, Harshil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Wave-PDE Nets: Trainable Wave-Equation Layers as an Alternative to Attention"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T17:52:52Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:03:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce Wave-PDE Nets, a neural architecture whose elementary operation is a differentiable simulation of the second-order wave equation. Each layer propagates its hidden state as a continuous field through a medium with trainable spatial velocity c(x) and damping γ(x). A symplectic spectral solver based on FFTs realises this propagation in O(nlog n) time. This oscillatory, global mechanism provides a powerful alternative to attention and first-order state-space models. We prove that a single Wave-PDE layer is a universal approximator. On language and vision benchmarks, Wave-PDE Nets match or exceed Transformer performance while demonstrating superior practical efficiency, reducing wall-clock time by up to 30% and peak memory by 25%. Ablation studies confirm the critical role of symplectic integration and a spectral Laplacian for stability and performance. Visualizations of the learned physical parameters reveal that the model learns intuitive strategies for information propagation. These results position Wave-PDE Nets as a computationally efficient and robust architecture with a strong physical inductive bias."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"PRICAI 2025 Oral, 9 pages, 3 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04304","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:11:14Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:11:14Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:11:14Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04293","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04293","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04293","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lingnan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Lingnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chong","familyName":"Feng","name":"Feng, Chong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kaiyuan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Kaiyuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liu","familyName":"Zhengyong","name":"Zhengyong, Liu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wenqiang","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Wenqiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fanqing","familyName":"Meng","name":"Meng, Fanqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Equipping Retrieval-Augmented Large Language Models with Document Structure Awareness"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T17:04:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:02:14Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While large language models (LLMs) demonstrate impressive capabilities, their reliance on parametric knowledge often leads to factual inaccuracies. Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) mitigates this by leveraging external documents, yet existing approaches treat retrieved passages as isolated chunks, ignoring valuable structure that is crucial for document organization. Motivated by this gap, we propose Retrieve-DocumentRoute-Read (RDR2), a novel framework that explicitly incorporates structural information throughout the RAG process. RDR2 employs an LLM-based router to dynamically navigate document structure trees, jointly evaluating content relevance and hierarchical relationships to assemble optimal evidence. Our key innovation lies in formulating document routing as a trainable task, with automatic action curation and structure-aware passage selection inspired by human reading strategies. Through comprehensive evaluation on five challenging datasets, RDR2 achieves state-of-the-art performance, demonstrating that explicit structural awareness significantly enhances RAG systems' ability to acquire and utilize knowledge, particularly in complex scenarios requiring multi-document synthesis."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04293","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:58Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:10:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04286","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04286","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04286","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harshil","familyName":"Vejendla","name":"Vejendla, Harshil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SliceMoE: Routing Embedding Slices Instead of Tokens for Fine-Grained and Balanced Transformer Scaling"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T16:57:32Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:02:05Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) layers scale transformers by routing tokens to a sparse subset of feed-forward experts. Token-level routing, however, assigns an entire semantic spectrum to each expert, creating capacity bottlenecks, load-balancing pathologies, and limited specialization. We introduce SliceMoE, an architecture that routes contiguous slices of a token's hidden vector. A d-dimensional embedding is partitioned into S slices, and for each slice, a lightweight shared router predicts the top-k experts. Experts operate on their assigned slices independently, and outputs are reassembled, maintaining per-token FLOP efficiency. Because slices from different tokens interleave within an expert, utilization is naturally smoother. We propose a slice-level capacity loss, cross-slice dropout, and efficient fused batched GEMM kernels. Experiments on WikiText-103 language modeling, WMT En-De translation, and three text-classification datasets show SliceMoE attains up to 1.7x faster inference than dense baselines, 12 to 18 percent lower perplexity than parameter-matched token-MoE, and improved expert balance, with interpretable expertise over syntactic versus semantic subspaces."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"EMNLP 2025 Main, 8 pages, 9 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04286","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:47Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:48Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:10:48Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04285","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04285","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04285","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karthik","familyName":"Viswanathan","name":"Viswanathan, Karthik","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sang Eon","familyName":"Park","name":"Park, Sang Eon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Probing Geometry of Next Token Prediction Using Cumulant Expansion of the Softmax Entropy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Statistical Mechanics (cond-mat.stat-mech)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (stat.ML)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Physical sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T16:55:58Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:02:02Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"We introduce a cumulant-expansion framework for quantifying how large language models (LLMs) internalize higher-order statistical structure during next-token prediction. By treating the softmax entropy of each layer's logit distribution as a perturbation around its \"center\" distribution, we derive closed-form cumulant observables that isolate successively higher-order correlations. Empirically, we track these cumulants in GPT-2 and Pythia models on Pile-10K prompts. (i) Structured prompts exhibit a characteristic rise-and-plateau profile across layers, whereas token-shuffled prompts remain flat, revealing the dependence of the cumulant profile on meaningful context. (ii) During training, all cumulants increase monotonically before saturating, directly visualizing the model's progression from capturing variance to learning skew, kurtosis, and higher-order statistical structures. (iii) Mathematical prompts show distinct cumulant signatures compared to general text, quantifying how models employ fundamentally different processing mechanisms for mathematical versus linguistic content. Together, these results establish cumulant analysis as a lightweight, mathematically grounded probe of feature-learning dynamics in high-dimensional neural networks."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"14 pages, 7 figures. Poster at HiLD 2025: 3rd Workshop on High-dimensional Learning Dynamics"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04285","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:46Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:10:47Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04274","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04274","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04274","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Damjan","familyName":"Fujs","name":"Fujs, Damjan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Damjan","familyName":"Vavpotič","name":"Vavpotič, Damjan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tomaž","familyName":"Hovelja","name":"Hovelja, Tomaž","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Marko","familyName":"Poženel","name":"Poženel, Marko","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Selecting Cybersecurity Requirements: Effects of LLM Use and Professional Software Development Experience"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"D.2; I.2; J.6; K.3; K.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T16:29:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:01:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study investigates how access to Large Language Models (LLMs) and varying levels of professional software development experience affect the prioritization of cybersecurity requirements for web applications. Twenty-three postgraduate students participated in a research study to prioritize security requirements (SRs) using the MoSCoW method and subsequently rated their proposed solutions against multiple evaluation criteria. We divided participants into two groups (one with and the other without access to LLM support during the task). Results showed no significant differences related to LLM use, suggesting that access to LLMs did not noticeably influence how participants evaluated cybersecurity solutions. However, statistically significant differences emerged between experience groups for certain criteria, such as estimated cost to develop a feature, perceived impact on user experience, and risk assessment related to non-implementation of the proposed feature. Participants with more professional experience tended to provide higher ratings for user experience impact and lower risk estimates."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages, 1 figure, 2 tables, presented at IARIA CYBER 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04274","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:10:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04268","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04268","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04268","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robin","familyName":"Algayres","name":"Algayres, Robin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Charles-Éric","familyName":"Saint-James","name":"Saint-James, Charles-Éric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mahi","familyName":"Luthra","name":"Luthra, Mahi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayi","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Jiayi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dongyan","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Dongyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Youssef","familyName":"Benchekroun","name":"Benchekroun, Youssef","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rashel","familyName":"Moritz","name":"Moritz, Rashel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Pino","name":"Pino, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emmanuel","familyName":"Dupoux","name":"Dupoux, Emmanuel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LongTail-Swap: benchmarking language models' abilities on rare words"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T16:17:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:00:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Children learn to speak with a low amount of data and can be taught new words on a few-shot basis, making them particularly data-efficient learners. The BabyLM challenge aims at exploring language model (LM) training in the low-data regime but uses metrics that concentrate on the head of the word distribution. Here, we introduce LongTail-Swap (LT-Swap), a benchmark that focuses on the tail of the distribution, i.e., measures the ability of LMs to learn new words with very little exposure, like infants do. LT-Swap is a pretraining corpus-specific test set of acceptable versus unacceptable sentence pairs that isolate semantic and syntactic usage of rare words. Models are evaluated in a zero-shot fashion by computing the average log probabilities over the two members of each pair. We built two such test sets associated with the 10M words and 100M words BabyLM training sets, respectively, and evaluated 16 models from the BabyLM leaderboard. Our results not only highlight the poor performance of language models on rare words but also reveal that performance differences across LM architectures are much more pronounced in the long tail than in the head. This offers new insights into which architectures are better at handling rare word generalization. We've also made the code publicly avail"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04268","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:10:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04261","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04261","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04261","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sicheng","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Sicheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifei","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yifei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haibin","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Haibin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Cong","familyName":"Zuo","name":"Zuo, Cong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"VortexPIA: Indirect Prompt Injection Attack against LLMs for Efficient Extraction of User Privacy"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T15:58:55Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:00:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have been widely deployed in Conversational AIs (CAIs), while exposing privacy and security threats. Recent research shows that LLM-based CAIs can be manipulated to extract private information from human users, posing serious security threats. However, the methods proposed in that study rely on a white-box setting that adversaries can directly modify the system prompt. This condition is unlikely to hold in real-world deployments. The limitation raises a critical question: can unprivileged attackers still induce such privacy risks in practical LLM-integrated applications? To address this question, we propose \\textsc{VortexPIA}, a novel indirect prompt injection attack that induces privacy extraction in LLM-integrated applications under black-box settings. By injecting token-efficient data containing false memories, \\textsc{VortexPIA} misleads LLMs to actively request private information in batches. Unlike prior methods, \\textsc{VortexPIA} allows attackers to flexibly define multiple categories of sensitive data. We evaluate \\textsc{VortexPIA} on six LLMs, covering both traditional and reasoning LLMs, across four benchmark datasets. The results show that \\textsc{VortexPIA} significantly outperforms baselines and achieves state-of-the-art (SOTA) performance. It also demonstrates efficient privacy requests, reduced token consumption, and enhanced robustness against defense mechanisms. We further validate \\textsc{VortexPIA} on multiple realistic open-source LLM-integrated applications, demonstrating its practical effectiveness."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04261","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:12Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:12Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:10:12Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04257","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04257","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04257","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanjie","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yanjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yiming","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yiming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentTypo: Adaptive Typographic Prompt Injection Attacks against Black-box Multimodal Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T15:46:56Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T01:00:08Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal agents built on large vision-language models (LVLMs) are increasingly deployed in open-world settings but remain highly vulnerable to prompt injection, especially through visual inputs. We introduce AgentTypo, a black-box red-teaming framework that mounts adaptive typographic prompt injection by embedding optimized text into webpage images. Our automatic typographic prompt injection (ATPI) algorithm maximizes prompt reconstruction by substituting captioners while minimizing human detectability via a stealth loss, with a Tree-structured Parzen Estimator guiding black-box optimization over text placement, size, and color. To further enhance attack strength, we develop AgentTypo-pro, a multi-LLM system that iteratively refines injection prompts using evaluation feedback and retrieves successful past examples for continual learning. Effective prompts are abstracted into generalizable strategies and stored in a strategy repository, enabling progressive knowledge accumulation and reuse in future attacks. Experiments on the VWA-Adv benchmark across Classifieds, Shopping, and Reddit scenarios show that AgentTypo significantly outperforms the latest image-based attacks such as AgentAttack. On GPT-4o agents, our image-only attack raises the success rate from 0.23 to 0.45, with consistent results across GPT-4V, GPT-4o-mini, Gemini 1.5 Pro, and Claude 3 Opus. In image+text settings, AgentTypo achieves 0.68 ASR, also outperforming the latest baselines. Our findings reveal that AgentTypo poses a practical and potent threat to multimodal agents and highlight the urgent need for effective defense."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"13 pages, 8 figures. Submitted to IEEE Transactions on Information Forensics \u0026amp; Security"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04257","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:10:06Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:10:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:10:06Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04246","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04246","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04246","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huiwon","familyName":"Jang","name":"Jang, Huiwon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sihyun","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Sihyun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Heeseung","familyName":"Kwon","name":"Kwon, Heeseung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hojin","familyName":"Jeon","name":"Jeon, Hojin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Younggyo","familyName":"Seo","name":"Seo, Younggyo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jinwoo","familyName":"Shin","name":"Shin, Jinwoo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"ContextVLA: Vision-Language-Action Model with Amortized Multi-Frame Context"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T15:29:57Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:59:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Leveraging temporal context is crucial for success in partially observable robotic tasks. However, prior work in behavior cloning has demonstrated inconsistent performance gains when using multi-frame observations. In this paper, we introduce ContextVLA, a policy model that robustly improves robotic task performance by effectively leveraging multi-frame observations. Our approach is motivated by the key observation that Vision-Language-Action models (VLA), i.e., policy models built upon a Vision-Language Model (VLM), more effectively utilize multi-frame observations for action generation. This suggests that VLMs' inherent temporal understanding capability enables them to extract more meaningful context from multi-frame observations. However, the high dimensionality of video inputs introduces significant computational overhead, making VLA training and inference inefficient. To address this, ContextVLA compresses past observations into a single context token, allowing the policy to efficiently leverage temporal context for action generation. Our experiments show that ContextVLA consistently improves over single-frame VLAs and achieves the benefits of full multi-frame training but with reduced training and inference times."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Project page: https://huiwon-jang.github.io/contextvla"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04246","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:09:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:09:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:09:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04239","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04239","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04239","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tongzhou","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Tongzhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhao","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maolin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Maolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chi","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Chi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiangyu","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Xiangyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Empowering Denoising Sequential Recommendation with Large Language Model Embeddings"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T15:10:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:59:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3746252.3761427","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Sequential recommendation aims to capture user preferences by modeling sequential patterns in user-item interactions. However, these models are often influenced by noise such as accidental interactions, leading to suboptimal performance. Therefore, to reduce the effect of noise, some works propose explicitly identifying and removing noisy items. However, we find that simply relying on collaborative information may result in an over-denoising problem, especially for cold items. To overcome these limitations, we propose a novel framework: Interest Alignment for Denoising Sequential Recommendation (IADSR) which integrates both collaborative and semantic information. Specifically, IADSR is comprised of two stages: in the first stage, we obtain the collaborative and semantic embeddings of each item from a traditional sequential recommendation model and an LLM, respectively. In the second stage, we align the collaborative and semantic embeddings and then identify noise in the interaction sequence based on long-term and short-term interests captured in the collaborative and semantic modalities. Our extensive experiments on four public datasets validate the effectiveness of the proposed framework and its compatibility with different sequential recommendation systems."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by CIKM2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04239","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:09:39Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:09:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:09:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04206","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04206","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04206","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanchen","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Hanchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowen","familyName":"Lv","name":"Lv, Bowen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xueqiao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xueqiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bohao","familyName":"Jing","name":"Jing, Bohao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iat Long","familyName":"Iong","name":"Iong, Iat Long","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhenyu","familyName":"Hou","name":"Hou, Zhenyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zehan","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Zehan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanyu","familyName":"Lai","name":"Lai, Hanyu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifan","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Yifan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Lu","name":"Lu, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hongning","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Hongning","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jie","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Jie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxiao","familyName":"Dong","name":"Dong, Yuxiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"AgentRL: Scaling Agentic Reinforcement Learning with a Multi-Turn, Multi-Task Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T13:40:01Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:57:26Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have sparked growing interest in building generalist agents that can learn through online interactions. However, applying reinforcement learning (RL) to train LLM agents in multi-turn, multi-task settings remains challenging due to lack of scalable infrastructure and stable training algorithms. In this work, we present the AgentRL framework for scalable multi-turn, multi-task agentic RL training. On the infrastructure side, AgentRL features a fully-asynchronous generation-training pipeline for efficient multi-turn RL. To support heterogeneous environment development in multi-task RL, we design a unified function-call based API interface, containerized environment development, and a centralized controller. On the algorithm side, we propose cross-policy sampling to encourage model exploration in multi-turn settings and task advantage normalization to stabilize multi-task training. Experiments show that AgentRL, trained on open LLMs across five agentic tasks, significantly outperforms GPT-5, Clause-Sonnet-4, DeepSeek-R1, and other open-source LLM agents. Multi-task training with AgentRL matches the best results among all task-specific models. AgentRL is open-sourced at https://github.com/THUDM/AgentRL. The algorithm and framework are adopted in building \\textsc{\\href{https://autoglm.zhipuai.cn}{AutoGLM}}."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04206","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:08:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:08:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:08:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04202","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04202","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04202","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haiquan","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Haiquan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"You","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, You","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yingjie","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Yingjie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yaqing","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yaqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quanming","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Quanming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spectral Alignment as Predictor of Loss Explosion in Neural Network Training"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T13:35:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:57:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Loss explosions in training deep neural networks can nullify multi-million dollar training runs. Conventional monitoring metrics like weight and gradient norms are often lagging and ambiguous predictors, as their values vary dramatically across different models and even between layers of the same model, making it difficult to establish a unified standard for detecting impending failure. We introduce Spectral Alignment (SA), a novel, theoretically-grounded metric that monitors the distributional alignment between layer inputs and the principal singular vectors of weight matrices. We show that a collapse in the sign diversity of this alignment is a powerful early predictor of representational collapse and training divergence. Empirical results on language models demonstrate that monitoring the SA distribution provides a significantly earlier and clearer warning of loss explosions than traditional scalar metrics. SA's low computational overhead makes it a practical tool for safeguarding model training."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"18 pages, 8 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04202","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:08:43Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:08:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:08:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04188","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04188","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04188","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shikang","familyName":"Zheng","name":"Zheng, Shikang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guantao","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Guantao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qinming","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Qinming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuqi","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Yuqi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lixuan","familyName":"He","name":"He, Lixuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chang","familyName":"Zou","name":"Zou, Chang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peiliang","familyName":"Cai","name":"Cai, Peiliang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiacheng","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiacheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Let Features Decide Their Own Solvers: Hybrid Feature Caching for Diffusion Transformers"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T13:01:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:56:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion Transformers offer state-of-the-art fidelity in image and video synthesis, but their iterative sampling process remains a major bottleneck due to the high cost of transformer forward passes at each timestep. To mitigate this, feature caching has emerged as a training-free acceleration technique that reuses or forecasts hidden representations. However, existing methods often apply a uniform caching strategy across all feature dimensions, ignoring their heterogeneous dynamic behaviors. Therefore, we adopt a new perspective by modeling hidden feature evolution as a mixture of ODEs across dimensions, and introduce HyCa, a Hybrid ODE solver inspired caching framework that applies dimension-wise caching strategies. HyCa achieves near-lossless acceleration across diverse domains and models, including 5.55 times speedup on FLUX, 5.56 times speedup on HunyuanVideo, 6.24 times speedup on Qwen-Image and Qwen-Image-Edit without retraining."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04188","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:08:23Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:08:24Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:08:24Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04147","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04147","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04147","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yifeng","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yifeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ziang","familyName":"Ji","name":"Ji, Ziang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuxuan","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Yuxuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Biqing","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Biqing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanlin","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Hanlin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Linfeng","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Linfeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Self Speculative Decoding for Diffusion Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:52:28Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:54:24Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diffusion-based Large Language Models (dLLMs) have emerged as a competitive alternative to autoregressive models, offering unique advantages through bidirectional attention and parallel generation paradigms. However, the generation results of current parallel decoding methods deviate from stepwise decoding, introducing potential performance degradation, which limits their practical deployment. To address this problem, we propose \\textbf{S}elf \\textbf{S}peculative \\textbf{D}ecoding (SSD), a lossless inference acceleration method that leverages the dLLM itself as both speculative decoding drafter and verifier without auxiliary modules. SSD introduces a self-drafting mechanism where the model generates predictions for multiple positions, then verifies them through hierarchical verification trees in a single forward pass. Unlike traditional speculative decoding that requires separate draft models, SSD eliminates model redundancy and memory overhead by exploiting the dLLM's inherent parallel prediction capability for multiple positions. This self-speculative approach allows the model to progressively verify and accept multiple tokens in a single forward pass. Our experiments demonstrate that SSD achieves up to 3.46$\\times$ speedup while keeping the output identical to stepwise decoding on open source models such as LLaDA and Dream. Code will be made publicly available on GitHub."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04147","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:23Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:07:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04145","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04145","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04145","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chenxin","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Chenxin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Elyas Asadi","familyName":"Shamsabadi","name":"Shamsabadi, Elyas Asadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaohui","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Zhaohui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luming","familyName":"Shen","name":"Shen, Luming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alireza Ahmadian Fard","familyName":"Fini","name":"Fini, Alireza Ahmadian Fard","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Dias-da-Costa","name":"Dias-da-Costa, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Automating construction safety inspections using a multi-modal vision-language RAG framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:48:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:54:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Conventional construction safety inspection methods are often inefficient as they require navigating through large volume of information. Recent advances in large vision-language models (LVLMs) provide opportunities to automate safety inspections through enhanced visual and linguistic understanding. However, existing applications face limitations including irrelevant or unspecific responses, restricted modal inputs and hallucinations. Utilisation of Large Language Models (LLMs) for this purpose is constrained by availability of training data and frequently lack real-time adaptability. This study introduces SiteShield, a multi-modal LVLM-based Retrieval-Augmented Generation (RAG) framework for automating construction safety inspection reports by integrating visual and audio inputs. Using real-world data, SiteShield outperformed unimodal LLMs without RAG with an F1 score of 0.82, hamming loss of 0.04, precision of 0.76, and recall of 0.96. The findings indicate that SiteShield offers a novel pathway to enhance information retrieval and efficiency in generating safety reports."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"33 pages, 11 figures, 7 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04145","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:19Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:07:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04139","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04139","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04139","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tim","familyName":"Bakkenes","name":"Bakkenes, Tim","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniel","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Daniel","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anton","familyName":"Johansson","name":"Johansson, Anton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fine Tuning Methods for Low-resource Languages"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:36:36Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:54:04Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rise of Large Language Models has not been inclusive of all cultures. The models are mostly trained on English texts and culture which makes them underperform in other languages and cultural contexts. By developing a generalizable method for preparing culturally relevant datasets and post-training the Gemma 2 model, this project aimed to increase the performance of Gemma 2 for an underrepresented language and showcase how others can do the same to unlock the power of Generative AI in their country and preserve their cultural heritage."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04139","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:10Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:11Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:07:11Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04136","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04136","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04136","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Umberto","familyName":"Cappellazzo","name":"Cappellazzo, Umberto","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Minsu","familyName":"Kim","name":"Kim, Minsu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pingchuan","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Pingchuan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honglie","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Honglie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xubo","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xubo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Stavros","familyName":"Petridis","name":"Petridis, Stavros","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maja","familyName":"Pantic","name":"Pantic, Maja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"MoME: Mixture of Matryoshka Experts for Audio-Visual Speech Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Audio and Speech Processing (eess.AS)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Sound (cs.SD)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:34:34Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:54:01Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have recently shown strong potential in audio-visual speech recognition (AVSR), but their high computational demands and sensitivity to token granularity limit their practicality in resource-constrained settings. Token compression methods can reduce inference cost, but they require fixing a compression rate in advance and produce a single fixed-length output, offering no flexibility to balance information density and efficiency at inference time. Matryoshka representation learning (MRL) addresses this by enabling a single model to operate across multiple token granularities, allowing compression rates to be adjusted dynamically. However, current MRL-based methods treat each scale independently during training, limiting cross-scale generalization, robustness at high compression, and interpretability. To overcome these limitations, we propose MoME (Mixture of Matryoshka Experts), a novel framework that integrates sparse Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) into MRL-based LLMs for AVSR. MoME augments a frozen LLM with top-k routed and shared experts, allowing dynamic capacity allocation across scales and modalities. A shared router promotes consistent expert activation across granularities, enabling compressed sequences to benefit from representations learned at lower compression. Experiments on LRS2 and LRS3 demonstrate that MoME achieves state-of-the-art performance across AVSR, ASR, and VSR tasks, while requiring significantly fewer parameters and maintaining robustness under noise. MoME unifies the adaptability of MRL with the efficiency of MoE, offering a scalable and interpretable solution for resource-aware speech recognition."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04136","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:06Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:06Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:07:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04135","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04135","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04135","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jingzhi","familyName":"Gong","name":"Gong, Jingzhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixin","familyName":"Bian","name":"Bian, Yixin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luis","familyName":"de la Cal","name":"de la Cal, Luis","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Giovanni","familyName":"Pinna","name":"Pinna, Giovanni","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anisha","familyName":"Uteem","name":"Uteem, Anisha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Williams","name":"Williams, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mar","familyName":"Zamorano","name":"Zamorano, Mar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Karine","familyName":"Even-Mendoza","name":"Even-Mendoza, Karine","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"W. B.","familyName":"Langdon","name":"Langdon, W. B.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hector","familyName":"Menendez","name":"Menendez, Hector","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Federica","familyName":"Sarro","name":"Sarro, Federica","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"GA4GC: Greener Agent for Greener Code via Multi-Objective Configuration Optimization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:34:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:54:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Coding agents powered by LLMs face critical sustainability and scalability challenges in industrial deployment, with single runs consuming over 100k tokens and incurring environmental costs that may exceed optimization benefits. This paper introduces GA4GC, the first framework to systematically optimize coding agent runtime (greener agent) and code performance (greener code) trade-offs by discovering Pareto-optimal agent hyperparameters and prompt templates. Evaluation on the SWE-Perf benchmark demonstrates up to 135x hypervolume improvement, reducing agent runtime by 37.7% while improving correctness. Our findings establish temperature as the most critical hyperparameter, and provide actionable strategies to balance agent sustainability with code optimization effectiveness in industrial deployment."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by SSBSE'25 Challenge Track"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04135","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:07:04Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:07:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:07:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04130","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04130","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04130","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Yang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yitao","familyName":"Liang","name":"Liang, Yitao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhouchen","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Zhouchen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"On the Limitations and Capabilities of Position Embeddings for Length Generalization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T10:08:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:53:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"In Transformers, Position Embeddings (PEs) significantly influence Length Generalization (LG) performance, yet their fundamental role remains unclear. In this work, we investigate the limitations and capabilities of PEs in achieving LG. We theoretically analyze PEs in Position-Only Linear Attentions (POLAs), introducing Linear Representation Complexity (LRC) to characterize when PEs enable LG. Our analysis shows that PEs do not expand computational capabilities but structure learned computations across positions. Extending to practical Transformers, we propose Sequential Representation Complexity (SRC) and conjecture that LG is possible if and only if SRC remains invariant across scales. We support this hypothesis with empirical evidence in various reasoning tasks. To enhance LG, we introduce Scale Hint, allowing flexible instance scaling, and a Learning-Based Position Embedding framework that automatically learns positional relations. Our work provides theoretical insights and practical strategies for improving LG in Transformers."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04130","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:06:57Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:06:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:06:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04122","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04122","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04122","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["East China Normal University, Shanghai, China"],"givenName":"Yingjing","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Yingjing","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["East China Normal University, Shanghai, China"],"givenName":"Zhichao","familyName":"Huang","name":"Huang, Zhichao","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["East China Normal University, Shanghai, China"],"givenName":"Junbin","familyName":"Ren","name":"Ren, Junbin","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["East China Normal University, Shanghai, China"],"givenName":"Haichuan","familyName":"Song","name":"Song, Haichuan","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["East China Normal University, Shanghai, China"],"givenName":"Yang","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Yang","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["South China University of Technology, Guangzhou, China"],"givenName":"Yuting","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Yuting","nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","affiliation":["South China University of Technology, Guangzhou, China"],"givenName":"Zhanpeng","familyName":"Jin","name":"Jin, Zhanpeng","nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Wrist2Finger: Sensing Fingertip Force for Force-Aware Hand Interaction with a Ring-Watch Wearable"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T09:54:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:53:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3746059.3747767","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Hand pose tracking is essential for advancing applications in human-computer interaction. Current approaches, such as vision-based systems and wearable devices, face limitations in portability, usability, and practicality. We present a novel wearable system that reconstructs 3D hand pose and estimates per-finger forces using a minimal ring-watch sensor setup. A ring worn on the finger integrates an inertial measurement unit (IMU) to capture finger motion, while a smartwatch-based single-channel electromyography (EMG) sensor on the wrist detects muscle activations. By leveraging the complementary strengths of motion sensing and muscle signals, our approach achieves accurate hand pose tracking and grip force estimation in a compact wearable form factor. We develop a dual-branch transformer network that fuses IMU and EMG data with cross-modal attention to predict finger joint positions and forces simultaneously. A custom loss function imposes kinematic constraints for smooth force variation and realistic force saturation. Evaluation with 20 participants performing daily object interaction gestures demonstrates an average Mean Per Joint Position Error (MPJPE) of 0.57 cm and a fingertip force estimation (RMSE: 0.213, r=0.76). We showcase our system in a real-time Unity application, enabling virtual hand interactions that respond to user-applied forces. This minimal, force-aware tracking system has broad implications for VR/AR, assistive prosthetics, and ergonomic monitoring."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"15 pages, 13 figures. Accepted at UIST 2025 (ACM Symposium on User Interface Software and Technology). Yingjing Xiao and Zhichao Huang contributed equally. Corresponding author: Yang Gao (gaoyang@cs.ecnu.edu.cn)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04122","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:06:45Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:06:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:06:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04096","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04096","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04096","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Tommy","familyName":"Mordo","name":"Mordo, Tommy","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sagie","familyName":"Dekel","name":"Dekel, Sagie","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Omer","familyName":"Madmon","name":"Madmon, Omer","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Moshe","familyName":"Tennenholtz","name":"Tennenholtz, Moshe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Oren","familyName":"Kurland","name":"Kurland, Oren","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"RLRF: Competitive Search Agent Design via Reinforcement Learning from Ranker Feedback"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Science and Game Theory (cs.GT)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T08:46:23Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:51:58Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Competitive search is a setting where document publishers modify them to improve their ranking in response to a query. Recently, publishers have increasingly leveraged LLMs to generate and modify competitive content. We introduce Reinforcement Learning from Ranker Feedback (RLRF), a framework that trains LLMs using preference datasets derived from ranking competitions. The goal of a publisher (LLM-based) agent is to optimize content for improved ranking while accounting for the strategies of competing agents. We generate the datasets using approaches that do not rely on human-authored data. We show that our proposed agents consistently and substantially outperform previously suggested approaches for LLM-based competitive document modification. We further show that our agents are effective with ranking functions they were not trained for (i.e., out of distribution) and they adapt to strategic opponents. These findings provide support to the significant potential of using reinforcement learning in competitive search."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04096","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:06:07Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:06:08Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:06:08Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04090","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04090","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04090","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nikita","familyName":"Gabdullin","name":"Gabdullin, Nikita","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Using predefined vector systems as latent space configuration for neural network supervised training on data with arbitrarily large number of classes"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T08:28:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:51:28Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Supervised learning (SL) methods are indispensable for neural network (NN) training used to perform classification tasks. While resulting in very high accuracy, SL training often requires making NN parameter number dependent on the number of classes, limiting their applicability when the number of classes is extremely large or unknown in advance. In this paper we propose a methodology that allows one to train the same NN architecture regardless of the number of classes. This is achieved by using predefined vector systems as the target latent space configuration (LSC) during NN training. We discuss the desired properties of target configurations and choose randomly perturbed vectors of An root system for our experiments. These vectors are used to successfully train encoders and visual transformers (ViT) on Cinic-10 and ImageNet-1K in low- and high-dimensional cases by matching NN predictions with the predefined vectors. Finally, ViT is trained on a dataset with 1.28 million classes illustrating the applicability of the method to training on datasets with extremely large number of classes. In addition, potential applications of LSC in lifelong learning and NN distillation are discussed illustrating versatility of the proposed methodology."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"28 pages, 12 figures, 10 tables, 12 equations, 1 algorithm"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04090","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:57Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:58Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:05:58Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04089","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04089","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04089","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yitong","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Yitong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Liu","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Liu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Baosheng","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Baosheng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiayan","familyName":"Qiu","name":"Qiu, Jiayan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xikai","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xikai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Likang","familyName":"Xiao","name":"Xiao, Likang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yixing","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yixing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Quan","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Quan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SPOGW: a Score-based Preference Optimization method via Group-Wise comparison for workflows"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T08:26:29Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:51:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have exhibited significant capabilities in addressing challenging problems throughout various fields, often through the use of agentic workflows that adhere to structured instructions and multi-step procedures. However, designing such workflows demands substantial manual effort, posing challenges to scalability and generalizability. Recent studies have aimed to minimize the human intervention needed for their construction, leading to advances in automated techniques for optimizing agentic workflows. However, current approaches are often constrained by their limited representational capacity, insufficient adaptability, weak scalability, and pairwise comparison paradigm -- issues that stem primarily from a dependence on discrete optimization techniques. To overcome these limitations, we introduce a new score-based preference approach, refereed as SPOGW, which operates directly on cardinal reward signals through group-wise comparison and enables more efficient and stable optimization in a continuous space. SPOGW incorporates Iterative offline GRPO (ioGRPO) with advantage-masked KL divergence (mKL), which regulates training update by placing greater emphasis on the advantageous regions of the policy response. In five benchmark datasets covering mathematical reasoning, coding, and question answering, SPOGW matches or exceeds the performance of current state-of-the-art approaches, presenting a viable and forward-looking methodology for automated generation and optimization of agentic workflows."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04089","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:56Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:05:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04081","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04081","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04081","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Honglin","familyName":"Lin","name":"Lin, Honglin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qizhi","familyName":"Pei","name":"Pei, Qizhi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xin","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Xin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhuoshi","familyName":"Pan","name":"Pan, Zhuoshi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juntao","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Juntao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Conghui","familyName":"He","name":"He, Conghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lijun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Lijun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Scaling Code-Assisted Chain-of-Thoughts and Instructions for Model Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Programming Languages (cs.PL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T07:59:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:51:06Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reasoning capability is pivotal for Large Language Models (LLMs) to solve complex tasks, yet achieving reliable and scalable reasoning remains challenging. While Chain-of-Thought (CoT) prompting has become a mainstream approach, existing methods often suffer from uncontrolled generation, insufficient quality, and limited diversity in reasoning paths. Recent efforts leverage code to enhance CoT by grounding reasoning in executable steps, but such methods are typically constrained to predefined mathematical problems, hindering scalability and generalizability. In this work, we propose Caco (Code-Assisted Chain-of-ThOught), a novel framework that automates the synthesis of high-quality, verifiable, and diverse instruction-CoT reasoning data through code-driven augmentation. Unlike prior work, Caco first fine-tunes a code-based CoT generator on existing math and programming solutions in a unified code format, then scales the data generation to a large amount of diverse reasoning traces. Crucially, we introduce automated validation via code execution and rule-based filtering to ensure logical correctness and structural diversity, followed by reverse-engineering filtered outputs into natural language instructions and language CoTs to enrich task adaptability. This closed-loop process enables fully automated, scalable synthesis of reasoning data with guaranteed executability. Experiments on our created Caco-1.3M dataset demonstrate that Caco-trained models achieve strong competitive performance on mathematical reasoning benchmarks, outperforming existing strong baselines. Further analysis reveals that Caco's code-anchored verification and instruction diversity contribute to superior generalization across unseen tasks. Our work establishes a paradigm for building self-sustaining, trustworthy reasoning systems without human intervention."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted by NeurIPS2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04081","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:05:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04078","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04078","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04078","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Han","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Han","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Wei","familyName":"Minn","name":"Minn, Wei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonghui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yonghui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiakun","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiakun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ferdian","familyName":"Thung","name":"Thung, Ferdian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Terry Yue","familyName":"Zhuo","name":"Zhuo, Terry Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lwin Khin","familyName":"Shar","name":"Shar, Lwin Khin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Debin","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Debin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"David","familyName":"Lo","name":"Lo, David","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Bamboo: LLM-Driven Discovery of API-Permission Mappings in the Android Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T07:50:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:50:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The permission mechanism in the Android Framework is integral to safeguarding the privacy of users by managing users' and processes' access to sensitive resources and operations. As such, developers need to be equipped with an in-depth understanding of API permissions to build robust Android apps. Unfortunately, the official API documentation by Android chronically suffers from imprecision and incompleteness, causing developers to spend significant effort to accurately discern necessary permissions. This potentially leads to incorrect permission declarations in Android app development, potentially resulting in security violations and app failures. Recent efforts in improving permission specification primarily leverage static and dynamic code analyses to uncover API-permission mappings within the Android framework. Yet, these methodologies encounter substantial shortcomings, including poor adaptability to Android SDK and Framework updates, restricted code coverage, and a propensity to overlook essential API-permission mappings in intricate codebases. This paper introduces a pioneering approach utilizing large language models (LLMs) for a systematic examination of API-permission mappings. In addition to employing LLMs, we integrate a dual-role prompting strategy and an API-driven code generation approach into our mapping discovery pipeline, resulting in the development of the corresponding tool, \\tool{}. We formulate three research questions to evaluate the efficacy of \\tool{} against state-of-the-art baselines, assess the completeness of official SDK documentation, and analyze the evolution of permission-required APIs across different SDK releases. Our experimental results reveal that \\tool{} identifies 2,234, 3,552, and 4,576 API-permission mappings in Android versions 6, 7, and 10 respectively, substantially outprforming existing baselines."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04078","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:39Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:05:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04076","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04076","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04076","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amin","familyName":"Vahidi-Moghaddam","name":"Vahidi-Moghaddam, Amin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sayed Pedram Haeri","familyName":"Boroujeni","name":"Boroujeni, Sayed Pedram Haeri","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Iman","familyName":"Jebellat","name":"Jebellat, Iman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ehsan","familyName":"Jebellat","name":"Jebellat, Ehsan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Niloufar","familyName":"Mehrabi","name":"Mehrabi, Niloufar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zhaojian","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Zhaojian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"From Shadow to Light: Toward Safe and Efficient Policy Learning Across MPC, DeePC, RL, and LLM Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T07:47:51Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:50:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/publicdomain/zero/1.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Zero v1.0 Universal","rightsIdentifier":"cc0-1.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"One of the main challenges in modern control applications, particularly in robot and vehicle motion control, is achieving accurate, fast, and safe movement. To address this, optimal control policies have been developed to enforce safety while ensuring high performance. Since basic first-principles models of real systems are often available, model-based controllers are widely used. Model predictive control (MPC) is a leading approach that optimizes performance while explicitly handling safety constraints. However, obtaining accurate models for complex systems is difficult, which motivates data-driven alternatives. ML-based MPC leverages learned models to reduce reliance on hand-crafted dynamics, while reinforcement learning (RL) can learn near-optimal policies directly from interaction data. Data-enabled predictive control (DeePC) goes further by bypassing modeling altogether, directly learning safe policies from raw input-output data. Recently, large language model (LLM) agents have also emerged, translating natural language instructions into structured formulations of optimal control problems. Despite these advances, data-driven policies face significant limitations. They often suffer from slow response times, high computational demands, and large memory needs, making them less practical for real-world systems with fast dynamics, limited onboard computing, or strict memory constraints. To address this, various technique, such as reduced-order modeling, function-approximated policy learning, and convex relaxations, have been proposed to reduce computational complexity. In this paper, we present eight such approaches and demonstrate their effectiveness across real-world applications, including robotic arms, soft robots, and vehicle motion control."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04076","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:36Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:05:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04071","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04071","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04071","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zitian","familyName":"Gao","name":"Gao, Zitian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Haoming","familyName":"Luo","name":"Luo, Haoming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lynx","familyName":"Chen","name":"Chen, Lynx","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jason Klein","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jason Klein","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ran","familyName":"Tao","name":"Tao, Ran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Joey","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Joey","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bryan","familyName":"Dai","name":"Dai, Bryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What Makes Diffusion Language Models Super Data Learners?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T07:22:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:50:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent studies have shown that diffusion language models achieve remarkable data efficiency under limited-data constraints, yet the underlying mechanisms remain unclear. In this work, we perform extensive ablation experiments to disentangle the sources of this efficiency. Our results show that random masking of input tokens plays the dominant role. We further show that similar gains can be obtained through in MLP dropout and weight decay, indicating that stochastic regularization broadly enhances data efficiency in multi-epoch training. Our code is available at https://github.com/zitian-gao/data-efficiency."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Technical report, work in progress"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04071","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:05:29Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:05:29Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:05:29Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04051","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04051","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04051","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lele","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Lele","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qile","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Qile","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ruofan","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Ruofan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guanhua","familyName":"Fang","name":"Fang, Guanhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Toward a unified framework for data-efficient evaluation of large language models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T06:13:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:49:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Evaluating large language models (LLMs) on comprehensive benchmarks is a cornerstone of their development, yet it's often computationally and financially prohibitive. While Item Response Theory (IRT) offers a promising path toward data-efficient evaluation by disentangling model capability from item difficulty, existing IRT-based methods are hampered by significant limitations. They are typically restricted to binary correctness metrics, failing to natively handle the continuous scores used in generative tasks, and they operate on single benchmarks, ignoring valuable structural knowledge like correlations across different metrics or benchmarks. To overcome these challenges, we introduce LEGO-IRT, a unified and flexible framework for data-efficient LLM evaluation. LEGO-IRT's novel design natively supports both binary and continuous evaluation metrics. Moreover, it introduces a factorized architecture to explicitly model and leverage structural knowledge, decomposing model ability estimates into a general component and structure-specific (e.g., per-metric or per-benchmark) components. Through extensive experiments involving $70$ LLMs across $5$ benchmarks, we show that LEGO-IRT achieves stable capability estimates using just $3\\%$ of the total evaluation items. We demonstrate that incorporating structural knowledge reduces estimation error by up to $10\\%$ and reveal that the latent abilities estimated by our framework may align more closely with human preferences."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"codes available at https://github.com/Rorschach1989/efficient-lm-eval"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04051","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:58Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:59Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:59Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04048","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04048","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04048","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aparna","familyName":"Nair-Kanneganti","name":"Nair-Kanneganti, Aparna","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Trevor J.","familyName":"Chan","name":"Chan, Trevor J.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shir","familyName":"Goldfinger","name":"Goldfinger, Shir","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emily","familyName":"Mackay","name":"Mackay, Emily","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Brian","familyName":"Anthony","name":"Anthony, Brian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alison","familyName":"Pouch","name":"Pouch, Alison","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Increasing LLM response trustworthiness using voting ensembles"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T06:02:44Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:49:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Despite huge advances, LLMs still lack convenient and reliable methods to quantify the uncertainty in their responses, making them difficult to trust in high-stakes applications. One of the simplest approaches to eliciting more accurate answers is to select the mode of many responses, a technique known as ensembling. In this work, we expand on typical ensembling approaches by looking at ensembles with a variable voting threshold. We introduce a theoretical framework for question answering and show that, by permitting ensembles to \"abstain\" from providing an answer when the dominant response falls short of the threshold, it is possible to dramatically increase the trustworthiness of the remaining answers. From this framework, we derive theoretical results as well as report experimental results on two problem domains: arithmetic problem solving and clinical-note question-answering. In both domains, we observe that large gains in answer trustworthiness can be achieved using highly restrictive voting ensembles, while incurring relatively modest reductions in response yield and accuracy. Due to this quality, voting ensembles may be particularly useful in applications - such as healthcare and data annotation - that require a high degree of certainty but which may not require that every question receive an automated answer."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04048","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:54Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:55Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:55Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04045","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04045","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04045","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yunfan","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yunfan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kathleen","familyName":"McKeown","name":"McKeown, Kathleen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Smaranda","familyName":"Muresan","name":"Muresan, Smaranda","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Exploring Chain-of-Thought Reasoning for Steerable Pluralistic Alignment"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T05:39:50Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:48:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Language Models (LLMs) are typically trained to reflect a relatively uniform set of values, which limits their applicability to tasks that require understanding of nuanced human perspectives. Recent research has underscored the importance of enabling LLMs to support steerable pluralism -- the capacity to adopt a specific perspective and align generated outputs with it. In this work, we investigate whether Chain-of-Thought (CoT) reasoning techniques can be applied to building steerable pluralistic models. We explore several methods, including CoT prompting, fine-tuning on human-authored CoT, fine-tuning on synthetic explanations, and Reinforcement Learning with Verifiable Rewards (RLVR). We evaluate these approaches using the Value Kaleidoscope and OpinionQA datasets. Among the methods studied, RLVR consistently outperforms others and demonstrates strong training sample efficiency. We further analyze the generated CoT traces with respect to faithfulness and safety."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACL EMNLP 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04045","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:50Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:51Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:51Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04041","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04041","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04041","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ayudh","familyName":"Saxena","name":"Saxena, Ayudh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Harsh","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Harsh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sandeep","familyName":"Routray","name":"Routray, Sandeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rishi Rajesh","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Rishi Rajesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Esha","familyName":"Pahwa","name":"Pahwa, Esha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SITCOM: Scaling Inference-Time COMpute for VLAs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Robotics (cs.RO)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T05:24:08Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:48:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Learning robust robotic control policies remains a major challenge due to the high cost of collecting labeled data, limited generalization to unseen environments, and difficulties in planning over long horizons. While Vision-Language-Action (VLA) models offer a promising solution by grounding natural language instructions into single-step control commands, they often lack mechanisms for lookahead and struggle with compounding errors in dynamic tasks. In this project, we introduce Scaling Inference-Time COMpute for VLAs (SITCOM), a framework that augments any pretrained VLA with model-based rollouts and reward-based trajectory selection, inspired by Model Predictive Control algorithm. SITCOM leverages a learned dynamics model to simulate multi-step action rollouts to select the best candidate plan for real-world execution, transforming one-shot VLAs into robust long-horizon planners. We develop an efficient transformer-based dynamics model trained on large-scale BridgeV2 data and fine-tuned on SIMPLER environments to bridge the Real2Sim gap, and score candidate rollouts using rewards from simulator. Through comprehensive evaluation across multiple tasks and settings in the SIMPLER environment, we demonstrate that SITCOM when combined with a good reward function can significantly improve task completion rate from 48% to 72% using trained dynamics model."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted at the NeurIPS 2025 Workshop on Space in Vision, Language, and Embodied AI (SpaVLE). *Equal contribution"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04041","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:44Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:45Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:45Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04039","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04039","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04039","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bin","familyName":"Lei","name":"Lei, Bin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nuo","familyName":"Xu","name":"Xu, Nuo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Payani","name":"Payani, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mingyi","familyName":"Hong","name":"Hong, Mingyi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chunhua","familyName":"Liao","name":"Liao, Chunhua","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Yu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Caiwen","familyName":"Ding","name":"Ding, Caiwen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"\\textsc{GUI-Spotlight}: Adaptive Iterative Focus Refinement for Enhanced GUI Visual Grounding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T05:15:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:48:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multimodal large language models (MLLMs) have markedly expanded the competence of graphical user-interface (GUI) systems, propelling them beyond controlled simulations into complex, real-world environments across diverse platforms. However, practical usefulness is still bounded by the reliability of visual grounding, i.e., mapping textual references to exact on-screen elements. This limitation prevents the system from accurately performing pointer-level actions such as clicking or dragging. To address it, we introduce GUI-Spotlight -- a model trained for image-grounded reasoning that dynamically invokes multiple specialized tools to iteratively narrow its focus to the relevant region of the screen, thereby substantially improving visual grounding accuracy. On the ScreenSpot-Pro benchmark, GUI-Spotlight trained with only 18.5K training samples achieves 52.8\\% accuracy, surpassing V2P-7B (50.6\\% with 9.6M training samples) and GTA-1-7B (50.1\\% with 1.56M training samples)."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04039","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:41Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:42Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:42Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04032","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04032","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04032","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Zirui","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Zirui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Braden","familyName":"Teitge","name":"Teitge, Braden","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jessalyn","familyName":"Holodinsky","name":"Holodinsky, Jessalyn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steve","familyName":"Drew","name":"Drew, Steve","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Small Language Models for Emergency Departments Decision Support: A Benchmark Study"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T04:46:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:48:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) have become increasingly popular in medical domains to assist physicians with a variety of clinical and operational tasks. Given the fast-paced and high-stakes environment of emergency departments (EDs), small language models (SLMs), characterized by a reduction in parameter count compared to LLMs, offer significant potential due to their inherent reasoning capability and efficient performance. This enables SLMs to support physicians by providing timely and accurate information synthesis, thereby improving clinical decision-making and workflow efficiency. In this paper, we present a comprehensive benchmark designed to identify SLMs suited for ED decision support, taking into account both specialized medical expertise and broad general problem-solving capabilities. In our evaluations, we focus on SLMs that have been trained on a mixture of general-domain and medical corpora. A key motivation for emphasizing SLMs is the practical hardware limitations, operational cost constraints, and privacy concerns in the typical real-world deployments. Our benchmark datasets include MedMCQA, MedQA-4Options, and PubMedQA, with the medical abstracts dataset emulating tasks aligned with real ED physicians' daily tasks. Experimental results reveal that general-domain SLMs surprisingly outperform their medically fine-tuned counterparts across these diverse benchmarks for ED. This indicates that for ED, specialized medical fine-tuning of the model may not be required."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to 2025 IEEE International Conference on Autonomous and Trusted Computing (ATC 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04032","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:31Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:32Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:32Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04031","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04031","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04031","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nelvin","familyName":"Tan","name":"Tan, Nelvin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James Asikin","familyName":"Cheung","name":"Cheung, James Asikin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yu-Ching","familyName":"Shih","name":"Shih, Yu-Ching","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amol","familyName":"Salunkhe","name":"Salunkhe, Amol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Does Using Counterfactual Help LLMs Explain Textual Importance in Classification?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T04:45:53Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:47:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large language models (LLMs) are becoming useful in many domains due to their impressive abilities that arise from large training datasets and large model sizes. More recently, they have been shown to be very effective in textual classification tasks, motivating the need to explain the LLMs' decisions. Motivated by practical constrains where LLMs are black-boxed and LLM calls are expensive, we study how incorporating counterfactuals into LLM reasoning can affect the LLM's ability to identify the top words that have contributed to its classification decision. To this end, we introduce a framework called the decision changing rate that helps us quantify the importance of the top words in classification. Our experimental results show that using counterfactuals can be helpful."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"8 pages, 2 figures"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04031","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:30Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:31Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:31Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04028","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04028","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04028","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xinhao","familyName":"Yao","name":"Yao, Xinhao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Lu","familyName":"Yu","name":"Yu, Lu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiaolin","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Xiaolin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Fengwei","familyName":"Teng","name":"Teng, Fengwei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qing","familyName":"Cui","name":"Cui, Qing","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jun","familyName":"Zhou","name":"Zhou, Jun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yong","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"The Debate on RLVR Reasoning Capability Boundary: Shrinkage, Expansion, or Both? A Two-Stage Dynamic View"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T04:31:33Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:47:50Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The ongoing debate on whether reinforcement learning with verifiable rewards (RLVR) expands or shrinks the reasoning capabilities of large language models (LLMs) remains unresolved. Some studies contend that RLVR mainly improves sampling efficiency but at the expense of diversity and exploratory capacity, resulting in capability boundary shrinkage. In contrast, others demonstrate that prolonged training can lead to the emergence of novel reasoning strategies, suggesting capability boundary expansion. To reconcile these contradictory findings, we theoretically and empirically show that both perspectives are partially valid-each aligning with a separate phase in an inherent two-stage probability mass dynamic: (1) Exploitation stage: initially, the model primarily samples explored high-reward and low-reward tokens, while rarely selecting the potentially optimal token. Positive advantage estimates increase the probability of high-reward tokens and decrease those of low-reward tokens, yet the optimal token's probability remains largely unchanged during this stage. (2) Exploration stage: as training advances, the growth rate of previously acquired high-reward tokens slows as their probabilities approach saturation. When a potentially optimal token-now receiving positive advantage estimates-is occasionally sampled, its probability increases, while those of the originally high-reward tokens decrease. This dynamic suggests that over-exploitation during the exploitation stage may lead to capability boundary shrinkage, whereas prolonged training into the exploration stage can promote an expansion of the reasoning capability boundary. Building upon our insights, we revisit the potential of only using relative negative gradients for prolonging training, providing a theoretical and empirical foundation for the development of more advanced reasoning capabilities."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04028","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:25Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:26Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:26Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04024","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04024","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04024","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuyan","familyName":"Bu","name":"Bu, Yuyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Qiang","familyName":"Sheng","name":"Sheng, Qiang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Juan","familyName":"Cao","name":"Cao, Juan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shaofei","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Shaofei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Peng","familyName":"Qi","name":"Qi, Peng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhui","familyName":"Shi","name":"Shi, Yuhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Beizhe","familyName":"Hu","name":"Hu, Beizhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Enhancing Fake News Video Detection via LLM-Driven Creative Process Simulation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T04:05:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:47:18Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3746252.3760933","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The emergence of fake news on short video platforms has become a new significant societal concern, necessitating automatic video-news-specific detection. Current detectors primarily rely on pattern-based features to separate fake news videos from real ones. However, limited and less diversified training data lead to biased patterns and hinder their performance. This weakness stems from the complex many-to-many relationships between video material segments and fabricated news events in real-world scenarios: a single video clip can be utilized in multiple ways to create different fake narratives, while a single fabricated event often combines multiple distinct video segments. However, existing datasets do not adequately reflect such relationships due to the difficulty of collecting and annotating large-scale real-world data, resulting in sparse coverage and non-comprehensive learning of the characteristics of potential fake news video creation. To address this issue, we propose a data augmentation framework, AgentAug, that generates diverse fake news videos by simulating typical creative processes. AgentAug implements multiple LLM-driven pipelines of four fabrication categories for news video creation, combined with an active learning strategy based on uncertainty sampling to select the potentially useful augmented samples during training. Experimental results on two benchmark datasets demonstrate that AgentAug consistently improves the performance of short video fake news detectors."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACM CIKM 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04024","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:19Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:20Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:20Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04023","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04023","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04023","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mizanur","familyName":"Rahman","name":"Rahman, Mizanur","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Amran","familyName":"Bhuiyan","name":"Bhuiyan, Amran","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammed Saidul","familyName":"Islam","name":"Islam, Mohammed Saidul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md Tahmid Rahman","familyName":"Laskar","name":"Laskar, Md Tahmid Rahman","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ridwan","familyName":"Mahbub","name":"Mahbub, Ridwan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ahmed","familyName":"Masry","name":"Masry, Ahmed","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shafiq","familyName":"Joty","name":"Joty, Shafiq","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Enamul","familyName":"Hoque","name":"Hoque, Enamul","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM-Based Data Science Agents: A Survey of Capabilities, Challenges, and Future Directions"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T04:04:27Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:47:17Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in large language models (LLMs) have enabled a new class of AI agents that automate multiple stages of the data science workflow by integrating planning, tool use, and multimodal reasoning across text, code, tables, and visuals. This survey presents the first comprehensive, lifecycle-aligned taxonomy of data science agents, systematically analyzing and mapping forty-five systems onto the six stages of the end-to-end data science process: business understanding and data acquisition, exploratory analysis and visualization, feature engineering, model building and selection, interpretation and explanation, and deployment and monitoring. In addition to lifecycle coverage, we annotate each agent along five cross-cutting design dimensions: reasoning and planning style, modality integration, tool orchestration depth, learning and alignment methods, and trust, safety, and governance mechanisms. Beyond classification, we provide a critical synthesis of agent capabilities, highlight strengths and limitations at each stage, and review emerging benchmarks and evaluation practices. Our analysis identifies three key trends: most systems emphasize exploratory analysis, visualization, and modeling while neglecting business understanding, deployment, and monitoring; multimodal reasoning and tool orchestration remain unresolved challenges; and over 90% lack explicit trust and safety mechanisms. We conclude by outlining open challenges in alignment stability, explainability, governance, and robust evaluation frameworks, and propose future research directions to guide the development of robust, trustworthy, low-latency, transparent, and broadly accessible data science agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Survey paper; 45 data science agents; under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04023","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:18Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04021","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04021","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04021","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kushal","familyName":"Vyas","name":"Vyas, Kushal","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ashok","familyName":"Veeraraghavan","name":"Veeraraghavan, Ashok","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Guha","familyName":"Balakrishnan","name":"Balakrishnan, Guha","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Fit Pixels, Get Labels: Meta-learned Implicit Networks for Image Segmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T04:03:17Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:47:12Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1007/978-3-032-04947-6_19","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Implicit neural representations (INRs) have achieved remarkable successes in learning expressive yet compact signal representations. However, they are not naturally amenable to predictive tasks such as segmentation, where they must learn semantic structures over a distribution of signals. In this study, we introduce MetaSeg, a meta-learning framework to train INRs for medical image segmentation. MetaSeg uses an underlying INR that simultaneously predicts per pixel intensity values and class labels. It then uses a meta-learning procedure to find optimal initial parameters for this INR over a training dataset of images and segmentation maps, such that the INR can simply be fine-tuned to fit pixels of an unseen test image, and automatically decode its class labels. We evaluated MetaSeg on 2D and 3D brain MRI segmentation tasks and report Dice scores comparable to commonly used U-Net models, but with $90\\%$ fewer parameters. MetaSeg offers a fresh, scalable alternative to traditional resource-heavy architectures such as U-Nets and vision transformers for medical image segmentation. Our project is available at https://kushalvyas.github.io/metaseg.html ."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"MICCAI 2025 (oral). Final peer-reviewed copy accessible at publisher DOI https://link.springer.com/chapter/10.1007/978-3-032-04947-6_19 . Project page, https://kushalvyas.github.io/metaseg.html"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04021","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:15Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:16Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:16Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04016","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04016","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04016","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Thanapol","familyName":"Popit","name":"Popit, Thanapol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Natthapath","familyName":"Rungseesiripak","name":"Rungseesiripak, Natthapath","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Monthol","familyName":"Charattrakool","name":"Charattrakool, Monthol","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saksorn","familyName":"Ruangtanusak","name":"Ruangtanusak, Saksorn","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Thai Semantic End-of-Turn Detection for Real-Time Voice Agents"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T03:31:59Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:55Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Fluid voice-to-voice interaction requires reliable and low-latency detection of when a user has finished speaking. Traditional audio-silence end-pointers add hundreds of milliseconds of delay and fail under hesitations or language-specific phenomena. We present, to our knowledge, the first systematic study of Thai text-only end-of-turn (EOT) detection for real-time agents. We compare zero-shot and few-shot prompting of compact LLMs to supervised fine-tuning of lightweight transformers. Using transcribed subtitles from the YODAS corpus and Thai-specific linguistic cues (e.g., sentence-final particles), we formulate EOT as a binary decision over token boundaries. We report a clear accuracy-latency tradeoff and provide a public-ready implementation plan. This work establishes a Thai baseline and demonstrates that small, fine-tuned models can deliver near-instant EOT decisions suitable for on-device agents."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"IEEE ICSEC 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04016","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:08Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:09Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:09Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04013","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04013","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04013","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiarui","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiarui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jivitesh","familyName":"Jain","name":"Jain, Jivitesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mona","familyName":"Diab","name":"Diab, Mona","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nishant","familyName":"Subramani","name":"Subramani, Nishant","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM Microscope: What Model Internals Reveal About Answer Correctness and Context Utilization"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T03:14:05Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:45Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Although large language models (LLMs) have tremendous utility, trustworthiness is still a chief concern: models often generate incorrect information with high confidence. While contextual information can help guide generation, identifying when a query would benefit from retrieved context and assessing the effectiveness of that context remains challenging. In this work, we operationalize interpretability methods to ascertain whether we can predict the correctness of model outputs from the model's activations alone. We also explore whether model internals contain signals about the efficacy of external context. We consider correct, incorrect, and irrelevant context and introduce metrics to distinguish amongst them. Experiments on six different models reveal that a simple classifier trained on intermediate layer activations of the first output token can predict output correctness with about 75% accuracy, enabling early auditing. Our model-internals-based metric significantly outperforms prompting baselines at distinguishing between correct and incorrect context, guarding against inaccuracies introduced by polluted context. These findings offer a lens to better understand the underlying decision-making processes of LLMs. Our code is publicly available at https://github.com/jiarui-liu/LLM-Microscope"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04013","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:04:04Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:04:05Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:04:05Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04001","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.04001","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.04001","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xuankang","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Xuankang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiangming","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Jiangming","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Named Entity Recognition in COVID-19 tweets with Entity Knowledge Augmentation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T02:22:26Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:46:11Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The COVID-19 pandemic causes severe social and economic disruption around the world, raising various subjects that are discussed over social media. Identifying pandemic-related named entities as expressed on social media is fundamental and important to understand the discussions about the pandemic. However, there is limited work on named entity recognition on this topic due to the following challenges: 1) COVID-19 texts in social media are informal and their annotations are rare and insufficient to train a robust recognition model, and 2) named entity recognition in COVID-19 requires extensive domain-specific knowledge. To address these issues, we propose a novel entity knowledge augmentation approach for COVID-19, which can also be applied in general biomedical named entity recognition in both informal text format and formal text format. Experiments carried out on the COVID-19 tweets dataset and PubMed dataset show that our proposed entity knowledge augmentation improves NER performance in both fully-supervised and few-shot settings. Our source code is publicly available: https://github.com/kkkenshi/LLM-EKA/tree/master"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Work in progress"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.04001","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:45Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:46Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:03:46Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03984","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03984","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03984","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kirandeep","familyName":"Kaur","name":"Kaur, Kirandeep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Preetam Prabhu Srikar","familyName":"Dammu","name":"Dammu, Preetam Prabhu Srikar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hideo","familyName":"Joho","name":"Joho, Hideo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chirag","familyName":"Shah","name":"Shah, Chirag","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Beyond Static Evaluation: Rethinking the Assessment of Personalized Agent Adaptability in Information Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-05T00:35:37Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:44:54Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[{"relationType":"IsVersionOf","relatedIdentifier":"10.1145/3767695.3769484","relatedIdentifierType":"DOI"}],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Personalized AI agents are becoming central to modern information retrieval, yet most evaluation methodologies remain static, relying on fixed benchmarks and one-off metrics that fail to reflect how users' needs evolve over time. These limitations hinder our ability to assess whether agents can meaningfully adapt to individuals across dynamic, longitudinal interactions. In this perspective paper, we propose a conceptual lens for rethinking evaluation in adaptive personalization, shifting the focus from static performance snapshots to interaction-aware, evolving assessments. We organize this lens around three core components: (1) persona-based user simulation with temporally evolving preference models; (2) structured elicitation protocols inspired by reference interviews to extract preferences in context; and (3) adaptation-aware evaluation mechanisms that measure how agent behavior improves across sessions and tasks. While recent works have embraced LLM-driven user simulation, we situate this practice within a broader paradigm for evaluating agents over time. To illustrate our ideas, we conduct a case study in e-commerce search using the PersonalWAB dataset. Beyond presenting a framework, our work lays a conceptual foundation for understanding and evaluating personalization as a continuous, user-centric endeavor."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03984","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":1,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:21Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:22Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:03:22Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03978","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03978","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03978","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Min Woo","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Min Woo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alejandro","familyName":"Lozano","name":"Lozano, Alejandro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Javier Gamazo","familyName":"Tejero","name":"Tejero, Javier Gamazo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Vishwesh","familyName":"Nath","name":"Nath, Vishwesh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xiao Xiao","familyName":"Sun","name":"Sun, Xiao Xiao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"James","familyName":"Burgess","name":"Burgess, James","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yuhui","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Yuhui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Kun","familyName":"Yuan","name":"Yuan, Kun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Robert","familyName":"Tibshirani","name":"Tibshirani, Robert","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sean","familyName":"Huver","name":"Huver, Sean","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Serena","familyName":"Yeung-Levy","name":"Yeung-Levy, Serena","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"No Tokens Wasted: Leveraging Long Context in Biomedical Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T23:38:18Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:44:39Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Embedding vision-language models (VLMs) are typically pretrained with short text windows (\u0026lt;77 tokens), which forces the truncation of long-format captions. Yet, the distribution of biomedical captions from large-scale open source literature reveals that a huge portion of captions far exceed 77 tokens. To this end, we investigate the impact of pretraining on long-format biomedical captions by extending the context length of text encoders in VLMs. We find that longer context (thus, enabling additional supervision provided in long-format captions) correlates with better retrieval and classification performance. Given this finding, we introduce BIOMEDICA-LongCAP, a dataset of 1M image-caption pairs enriched with context-aware descriptions from full-text articles, providing longer and additional textual supervision. Using BIOMEDICA-LongCAP, we train BMC-LongCLIP, a long-context biomedical VLM with a text encoder supporting windows of up to 512 tokens. Our model extends context capacity by 6.6x, reducing token waste from 55% to just 2.2%. On long-caption retrieval benchmarks, BMC-LongCLIP achieves up to +30% absolute gains in Recall@1 and +2% average improvements in classification, while also converging faster than short-context. Our results demonstrate that long-context modeling is a promising direction for advancing biomedical VLMs."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03978","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:12Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:03:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03971","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03971","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03971","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jatin","familyName":"Prakash","name":"Prakash, Jatin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Anirudh","familyName":"Buvanesh","name":"Buvanesh, Anirudh","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"What Can You Do When You Have Zero Rewards During RL?"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T23:10:38Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:44:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Reinforcement learning (RL) with outcome-based rewards has proven effective for improving large language models (LLMs) on complex reasoning tasks. However, its success often depends on the base model occasionally sampling correct solutions. When no correct solutions are sampled, training encounters a zero-reward barrier where learning stalls due to zero gradients. We study this scenario through the graph search task introduced in Bachmann et al. (2024) and evaluate recent methods that incorporate desirable components such as dense rewards, diversity incentives, and improved credit assignment. Our experiments show that none of these approaches overcome the zero-reward barrier if the base model never produces a correct answer. In contrast, we find that a simple data-centric intervention of adding easier samples to the training set enables the model to eventually solve the original hard task despite starting from zero reward. Importantly, this succeeds without modifying the RL algorithm itself. Because official implementations of several baselines were unavailable, we developed our own, which allowed us to conduct a detailed analysis of their failure modes. We release these implementations to support further research at: https://github.com/rl4reasoning/rl-baselines"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03971","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:03:02Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:03:03Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:03:03Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03965","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03965","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03965","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dong","familyName":"Shu","name":"Shu, Dong","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yanguang","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Yanguang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huopu","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Huopu","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mengnan","familyName":"Du","name":"Du, Mengnan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"FinCall-Surprise: A Large Scale Multi-modal Benchmark for Earning Surprise Prediction"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T22:33:20Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:44:13Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Predicting corporate earnings surprises is a profitable yet challenging task, as accurate forecasts can inform significant investment decisions. However, progress in this domain has been constrained by a reliance on expensive, proprietary, and text-only data, limiting the development of advanced models. To address this gap, we introduce \\textbf{FinCall-Surprise} (Financial Conference Call for Earning Surprise Prediction), the first large-scale, open-source, and multi-modal dataset for earnings surprise prediction. Comprising 2,688 unique corporate conference calls from 2019 to 2021, our dataset features word-to-word conference call textual transcripts, full audio recordings, and corresponding presentation slides. We establish a comprehensive benchmark by evaluating 26 state-of-the-art unimodal and multi-modal LLMs. Our findings reveal that (1) while many models achieve high accuracy, this performance is often an illusion caused by significant class imbalance in the real-world data. (2) Some specialized financial models demonstrate unexpected weaknesses in instruction-following and language generation. (3) Although incorporating audio and visual modalities provides some performance gains, current models still struggle to leverage these signals effectively. These results highlight critical limitations in the financial reasoning capabilities of existing LLMs and establish a challenging new baseline for future research."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03965","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:02:53Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:02:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:02:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03962","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03962","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03962","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hanzhe","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Hanzhe","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jiajun","familyName":"Wu","name":"Wu, Jiajun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jialin","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Jialin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Henry","familyName":"Leung","name":"Leung, Henry","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Steve","familyName":"Drew","name":"Drew, Steve","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"SPEAR: Soft Prompt Enhanced Anomaly Recognition for Time Series Data"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T22:20:48Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:44:00Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Time series anomaly detection plays a crucial role in a wide range of fields, such as healthcare and internet traffic monitoring. The emergence of large language models (LLMs) offers new opportunities for detecting anomalies in the ubiquitous time series data. Traditional approaches struggle with variable-length time series sequences and context-based anomalies. We propose Soft Prompt Enhanced Anomaly Recognition (SPEAR), a novel approach to leverage LLMs for anomaly detection with soft prompts and quantization. Our methodology involves quantizing and transforming the time series data into input embeddings and combining them with learnable soft prompt embeddings. These combined embeddings are then fed into a frozen LLM. The soft prompts are updated iteratively based on a cross-entropy loss, allowing the model to adapt to time series anomaly detection. The use of soft prompts helps adapt LLMs effectively to time series tasks, while quantization ensures optimal handling of sequences, as LLMs are designed to handle discrete sequences. Our experimental results demonstrate that soft prompts effectively increase LLMs' performance in downstream tasks regarding time series anomaly detection."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to 2025 IEEE International Conference on Autonomous and Trusted Computing (ATC 2025)"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03962","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:02:49Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:02:50Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:02:50Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03955","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03955","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03955","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sameep","familyName":"Vani","name":"Vani, Sameep","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shreyas","familyName":"Jena","name":"Jena, Shreyas","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Maitreya","familyName":"Patel","name":"Patel, Maitreya","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chitta","familyName":"Baral","name":"Baral, Chitta","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Somak","familyName":"Aditya","name":"Aditya, Somak","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yezhou","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Yezhou","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Harnessing Synthetic Preference Data for Enhancing Temporal Understanding of Video-LLMs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T21:48:40Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:43:44Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"While Video Large Language Models (Video-LLMs) have demonstrated remarkable performance across general video understanding benchmarks-particularly in video captioning and descriptive tasks-they consistently underperform on tasks that require fine-grained temporal understanding. This limitation arises due to the lack of visual complexity and temporal nuance in current fine-tuning datasets, leading these models to rely heavily on language-based reasoning rather than truly understanding video dynamics. In this work, we propose TimeWarp, a systematic method to create a targeted synthetic temporal dataset to fine-tune the model's responses to encourage it to focus on the given input video. We introduce a large-scale preference dataset, created using TimeWarp, that captures intricate temporal dynamics often overlooked, grounding the model's responses to visual and temporal information. We demonstrate that when our method is applied to existing models, it significantly improves performance on temporal understanding benchmarks, highlighting the effectiveness of our proposed datasets in advancing temporal understanding in Video-LLMs, resulting in an absolute improvement in performance across seven benchmarks. Code is available at https://github.com/sameepv21/timewarp."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 9 figures, 6 tables. Presents TimeWarp, a synthetic preference data framework to improve temporal understanding in Video-LLMs, showing consistent gains across seven benchmarks. Includes supplementary material in the Appendix"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03955","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:02:39Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:02:40Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:02:40Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03930","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03930","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03930","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huascar","familyName":"Sanchez","name":"Sanchez, Huascar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Briland","familyName":"Hitaj","name":"Hitaj, Briland","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"LLM Chemistry Estimation for Multi-LLM Recommendation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T20:21:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:42:03Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Multi-LLM collaboration promises accurate, robust, and context-aware solutions, yet existing approaches rely on implicit selection and output assessment without analyzing whether collaborating models truly complement or conflict. We introduce LLM Chemistry -- a framework that measures when LLM combinations exhibit synergistic or antagonistic behaviors that shape collective performance beyond individual capabilities. We formalize the notion of chemistry among LLMs, propose algorithms that quantify it by analyzing interaction dependencies, and recommend optimal model ensembles accordingly. Our theoretical analysis shows that chemistry among collaborating LLMs is most evident under heterogeneous model profiles, with its outcome impact shaped by task type, group size, and complexity. Evaluation on classification, summarization, and program repair tasks provides initial evidence for these task-dependent effects, thereby reinforcing our theoretical results. This establishes LLM Chemistry as both a diagnostic factor in multi-LLM systems and a foundation for ensemble recommendation."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"20 pages, 5 figures, 5 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03930","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:02:02Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:02:02Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:02:02Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03926","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03926","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03926","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Alexander","familyName":"Kopte","name":"Kopte, Alexander","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"André","familyName":"Kaup","name":"Kaup, André","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Sliding Window Attention for Learned Video Compression"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T20:11:43Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:41:53Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"To manage the complexity of transformers in video compression, local attention mechanisms are a practical necessity. The common approach of partitioning frames into patches, however, creates architectural flaws like irregular receptive fields. When adapted for temporal autoregressive models, this paradigm, exemplified by the Video Compression Transformer (VCT), also necessitates computationally redundant overlapping windows. This work introduces 3D Sliding Window Attention (SWA), a patchless form of local attention. By enabling a decoder-only architecture that unifies spatial and temporal context processing, and by providing a uniform receptive field, our method significantly improves rate-distortion performance, achieving Bjørntegaard Delta-rate savings of up to 18.6 % against the VCT baseline. Simultaneously, by eliminating the need for overlapping windows, our method reduces overall decoder complexity by a factor of 2.8, while its entropy model is nearly 3.5 times more efficient. We further analyze our model's behavior and show that while it benefits from long-range temporal context, excessive context can degrade performance."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted for PCS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03926","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:55Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:56Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:01:56Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03921","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03921","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03921","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arushi","familyName":"Dashore","name":"Dashore, Arushi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Aryan","familyName":"Anumala","name":"Anumala, Aryan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Emily","familyName":"Hui","name":"Hui, Emily","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Olivia","familyName":"Yang","name":"Yang, Olivia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Talking Tennis: Language Feedback from 3D Biomechanical Action Recognition"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Human-Computer Interaction (cs.HC)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"I.2.10; I.5.4; I.2.7","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"ACM"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T19:55:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:41:40Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Automated tennis stroke analysis has advanced significantly with the integration of biomechanical motion cues alongside deep learning techniques, enhancing stroke classification accuracy and player performance evaluation. Despite these advancements, existing systems often fail to connect biomechanical insights with actionable language feedback that is both accessible and meaningful to players and coaches. This research project addresses this gap by developing a novel framework that extracts key biomechanical features (such as joint angles, limb velocities, and kinetic chain patterns) from motion data using Convolutional Neural Network Long Short-Term Memory (CNN-LSTM)-based models. These features are analyzed for relationships influencing stroke effectiveness and injury risk, forming the basis for feedback generation using large language models (LLMs). Leveraging the THETIS dataset and feature extraction techniques, our approach aims to produce feedback that is technically accurate, biomechanically grounded, and actionable for end-users. The experimental setup evaluates this framework on classification performance and interpretability, bridging the gap between explainable AI and sports biomechanics."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"10 pages, 4 figures, 2 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03921","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:48Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:49Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:01:49Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03914","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03914","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03914","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yonnel Chen Kuang","familyName":"Piao","name":"Piao, Yonnel Chen Kuang","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jean Carlors","familyName":"Paul","name":"Paul, Jean Carlors","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Leuson","familyName":"Da Silva","name":"Da Silva, Leuson","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arghavan Moradi","familyName":"Dakhel","name":"Dakhel, Arghavan Moradi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Hamdaqa","name":"Hamdaqa, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Foutse","familyName":"Khomh","name":"Khomh, Foutse","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Refactoring with LLMs: Bridging Human Expertise and Machine Understanding"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T19:40:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:41:23Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Code refactoring is a fundamental software engineering practice aimed at improving code quality and maintainability. Despite its importance, developers often neglect refactoring due to the significant time, effort, and resources it requires, as well as the lack of immediate functional rewards. Although several automated refactoring tools have been proposed, they remain limited in supporting a broad spectrum of refactoring types. In this study, we explore whether instruction strategies inspired by human best-practice guidelines can enhance the ability of Large Language Models (LLMs) to perform diverse refactoring tasks automatically. Leveraging the instruction-following and code comprehension capabilities of state-of-the-art LLMs (e.g., GPT-mini and DeepSeek-V3), we draw on Martin Fowler's refactoring guidelines to design multiple instruction strategies that encode motivations, procedural steps, and transformation objectives for 61 well-known refactoring types. We evaluate these strategies on benchmark examples and real-world code snippets from GitHub projects. Our results show that instruction designs grounded in Fowler's guidelines enable LLMs to successfully perform all benchmark refactoring types and preserve program semantics in real-world settings, an essential criterion for effective refactoring. Moreover, while descriptive instructions are more interpretable to humans, our results show that rule-based instructions often lead to better performance in specific scenarios. Interestingly, allowing models to focus on the overall goal of refactoring, rather than prescribing a fixed transformation type, can yield even greater improvements in code quality."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"43 pages, 2 figures, 9 tables"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03914","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":1,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:38Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:39Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:01:39Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03913","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03913","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03913","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad Amin","familyName":"Abbasi","name":"Abbasi, Mohammad Amin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hassan","familyName":"Naderi","name":"Naderi, Hassan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PsycholexTherapy: Simulating Reasoning in Psychotherapy with Small Language Models in Persian"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T19:40:10Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:41:22Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial Share Alike 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-sa-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This study presents PsychoLexTherapy, a framework for simulating psychotherapeutic reasoning in Persian using small language models (SLMs). The framework tackles the challenge of developing culturally grounded, therapeutically coherent dialogue systems with structured memory for multi-turn interactions in underrepresented languages. To ensure privacy and feasibility, PsychoLexTherapy is optimized for on-device deployment, enabling use without external servers. Development followed a three-stage process: (i) assessing SLMs psychological knowledge with PsychoLexEval; (ii) designing and implementing the reasoning-oriented PsychoLexTherapy framework; and (iii) constructing two evaluation datasets-PsychoLexQuery (real Persian user questions) and PsychoLexDialogue (hybrid simulated sessions)-to benchmark against multiple baselines. Experiments compared simple prompting, multi-agent debate, and structured therapeutic reasoning paths. Results showed that deliberate model selection balanced accuracy, efficiency, and privacy. On PsychoLexQuery, PsychoLexTherapy outperformed all baselines in automatic LLM-as-a-judge evaluation and was ranked highest by human evaluators in a single-turn preference study. In multi-turn tests with PsychoLexDialogue, the long-term memory module proved essential: while naive history concatenation caused incoherence and information loss, the full framework achieved the highest ratings in empathy, coherence, cultural fit, and personalization. Overall, PsychoLexTherapy establishes a practical, privacy-preserving, and culturally aligned foundation for Persian psychotherapy simulation, contributing novel datasets, a reproducible evaluation pipeline, and empirical insights into structured memory for therapeutic reasoning."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03913","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:36Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:37Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:01:37Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03903","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03903","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03903","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Md.","familyName":"Atabuzzaman","name":"Atabuzzaman, Md.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Andrew","familyName":"Zhang","name":"Zhang, Andrew","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chris","familyName":"Thomas","name":"Thomas, Chris","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Zero-Shot Fine-Grained Image Classification Using Large Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T18:56:41Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:40:49Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs) have demonstrated impressive performance on vision-language reasoning tasks. However, their potential for zero-shot fine-grained image classification, a challenging task requiring precise differentiation between visually similar categories, remains underexplored. We present a novel method that transforms zero-shot fine-grained image classification into a visual question-answering framework, leveraging LVLMs' comprehensive understanding capabilities rather than relying on direct class name generation. We enhance model performance through a novel attention intervention technique. We also address a key limitation in existing datasets by developing more comprehensive and precise class description benchmarks. We validate the effectiveness of our method through extensive experimentation across multiple fine-grained image classification benchmarks. Our proposed method consistently outperforms the current state-of-the-art (SOTA) approach, demonstrating both the effectiveness of our method and the broader potential of LVLMs for zero-shot fine-grained classification tasks. Code and Datasets: https://github.com/Atabuzzaman/Fine-grained-classification"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to EMNLP 2025 Findings"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03903","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:22Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:23Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:01:23Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03902","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03902","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03902","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rana Nameer Hussain","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Rana Nameer Hussain","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dawood","familyName":"Wasif","name":"Wasif, Dawood","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Jin-Hee","familyName":"Cho","name":"Cho, Jin-Hee","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ali","familyName":"Butt","name":"Butt, Ali","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Multi-Agent Code-Orchestrated Generation for Reliable Infrastructure-as-Code"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T18:55:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:40:48Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The increasing complexity of cloud-native infrastructure has made Infrastructure-as-Code (IaC) essential for reproducible and scalable deployments. While large language models (LLMs) have shown promise in generating IaC snippets from natural language prompts, their monolithic, single-pass generation approach often results in syntactic errors, policy violations, and unscalable designs. In this paper, we propose MACOG (Multi-Agent Code-Orchestrated Generation), a novel multi-agent LLM-based architecture for IaC generation that decomposes the task into modular subtasks handled by specialized agents: Architect, Provider Harmonizer, Engineer, Reviewer, Security Prover, Cost and Capacity Planner, DevOps, and Memory Curator. The agents interact via a shared-blackboard, finite-state orchestrator layer, and collectively produce Terraform configurations that are not only syntactically valid but also policy-compliant and semantically coherent. To ensure infrastructure correctness and governance, we incorporate Terraform Plan for execution validation and Open Policy Agent (OPA) for customizable policy enforcement. We evaluate MACOG using the IaC-Eval benchmark, where MACOG is the top enhancement across models, e.g., GPT-5 improves from 54.90 (RAG) to 74.02 and Gemini-2.5 Pro from 43.56 to 60.13, with concurrent gains on BLEU, CodeBERTScore, and an LLM-judge metric. Ablations show constrained decoding and deploy feedback are critical: removing them drops IaC-Eval to 64.89 and 56.93, respectively."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03902","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:01:20Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:01:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:01:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03886","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03886","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03886","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seil","familyName":"Kang","name":"Kang, Seil","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Woojung","familyName":"Han","name":"Han, Woojung","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Dayun","familyName":"Ju","name":"Ju, Dayun","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Seong Jae","familyName":"Hwang","name":"Hwang, Seong Jae","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Rare Text Semantics Were Always There in Your Diffusion Transformer"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T17:41:24Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:39:56Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Starting from flow- and diffusion-based transformers, Multi-modal Diffusion Transformers (MM-DiTs) have reshaped text-to-vision generation, gaining acclaim for exceptional visual fidelity. As these models advance, users continually push the boundary with imaginative or rare prompts, which advanced models still falter in generating, since their concepts are often too scarce to leave a strong imprint during pre-training. In this paper, we propose a simple yet effective intervention that surfaces rare semantics inside MM-DiTs without additional training steps, data, denoising-time optimization, or reliance on external modules (e.g., large language models). In particular, the joint-attention mechanism intrinsic to MM-DiT sequentially updates text embeddings alongside image embeddings throughout transformer blocks. We find that by mathematically expanding representational basins around text token embeddings via variance scale-up before the joint-attention blocks, rare semantics clearly emerge in MM-DiT's outputs. Furthermore, our results generalize effectively across text-to-vision tasks, including text-to-image, text-to-video, and text-driven image editing. Our work invites generative models to reveal the semantics that users intend, once hidden yet ready to surface."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Accepted to NeurIPS 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03886","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:00:56Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:00:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:00:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03873","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03873","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03873","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Saja","familyName":"Al-Dabet","name":"Al-Dabet, Saja","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sherzod","familyName":"Turaev","name":"Turaev, Sherzod","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nazar","familyName":"Zaki","name":"Zaki, Nazar","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arif O.","familyName":"Khan","name":"Khan, Arif O.","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Luai","familyName":"Eldweik","name":"Eldweik, Luai","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"PoseGaze-AHP: A Knowledge-Based 3D Dataset for AI-Driven Ocular and Postural Diagnosis"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T16:50:30Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:39:15Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Diagnosing ocular-induced abnormal head posture (AHP) requires a comprehensive analysis of both head pose and ocular movements. However, existing datasets focus on these aspects separately, limiting the development of integrated diagnostic approaches and restricting AI-driven advancements in AHP analysis. To address this gap, we introduce PoseGaze-AHP, a novel 3D dataset that synchronously captures head pose and gaze movement information for ocular-induced AHP assessment. Structured clinical data were extracted from medical literature using large language models (LLMs) through an iterative process with the Claude 3.5 Sonnet model, combining stepwise, hierarchical, and complex prompting strategies. The extracted records were systematically imputed and transformed into 3D representations using the Neural Head Avatar (NHA) framework. The dataset includes 7,920 images generated from two head textures, covering a broad spectrum of ocular conditions. The extraction method achieved an overall accuracy of 91.92%, demonstrating its reliability for clinical dataset construction. PoseGaze-AHP is the first publicly available resource tailored for AI-driven ocular-induced AHP diagnosis, supporting the development of accurate and privacy-compliant diagnostic tools."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This is a preprint version of a manuscript under review. All rights reserved by the authors"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03873","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:00:35Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:00:36Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:00:36Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03863","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03863","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03863","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Arina","familyName":"Kharlamova","name":"Kharlamova, Arina","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Bowei","familyName":"He","name":"He, Bowei","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Chen","familyName":"Ma","name":"Ma, Chen","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Xue","familyName":"Liu","name":"Liu, Xue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Spatial CAPTCHA: Generatively Benchmarking Spatial Reasoning for Human-Machine Differentiation"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Cryptography and Security (cs.CR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T16:19:21Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:38:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Online services rely on CAPTCHAs as a first line of defense against automated abuse, yet recent advances in multi-modal large language models (MLLMs) have eroded the effectiveness of conventional designs that focus on text recognition or 2D image understanding. To address this challenge, we present Spatial CAPTCHA, a novel human-verification framework that leverages fundamental differences in spatial reasoning between humans and MLLMs. Unlike existing CAPTCHAs which rely on low-level perception tasks that are vulnerable to modern AI, Spatial CAPTCHA generates dynamic questions requiring geometric reasoning, perspective-taking, occlusion handling, and mental rotation. These skills are intuitive for humans but difficult for state-of-the-art (SOTA) AI systems. The system employs a procedural generation pipeline with constraint-based difficulty control, automated correctness verification, and human-in-the-loop validation to ensure scalability, robustness, and adaptability. Evaluation on a corresponding benchmark, Spatial-CAPTCHA-Bench, demonstrates that humans vastly outperform 10 state-of-the-art MLLMs, with the best model achieving only 31.0% Pass@1 accuracy. Furthermore, we compare Spatial CAPTCHA with Google reCAPTCHA, which confirms its effectiveness as both a security mechanism and a diagnostic tool for spatial reasoning in AI."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Submitted to ICLR 2026"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03863","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:00:20Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:00:21Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:00:21Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03862","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03862","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03862","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Nathalia","familyName":"Nascimento","name":"Nascimento, Nathalia","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Everton","familyName":"Guimaraes","name":"Guimaraes, Everton","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Paulo","familyName":"Alencar","name":"Alencar, Paulo","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Designing Empirical Studies on LLM-Based Code Generation: Towards a Reference Framework"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Software Engineering (cs.SE)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"500","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"MSC"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T16:15:54Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:38:38Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"The rise of large language models (LLMs) has introduced transformative potential in automated code generation, addressing a wide range of software engineering challenges. However, empirical evaluation of LLM-based code generation lacks standardization, with studies varying widely in goals, tasks, and metrics, which limits comparability and reproducibility. In this paper, we propose a theoretical framework for designing and reporting empirical studies on LLM-based code generation. The framework is grounded in both our prior experience conducting such experiments and a comparative analysis of key similarities and differences among recent studies. It organizes evaluation around core components such as problem sources, quality attributes, and metrics, supporting structured and systematic experimentation. We demonstrate its applicability through representative case mappings and identify opportunities for refinement. Looking forward, we plan to evolve the framework into a more robust and mature tool for standardizing LLM evaluation across software engineering contexts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"5 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03862","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:00:19Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:00:19Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:00:19Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03859","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03859","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03859","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raghav","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Raghav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manan","familyName":"Mehta","name":"Mehta, Manan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Adaptive and Explainable AI Agents for Anomaly Detection in Critical IoT Infrastructure using LLM-Enhanced Contextual Reasoning"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T16:12:45Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:38:29Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Ensuring that critical IoT systems function safely and smoothly depends a lot on finding anomalies quickly. As more complex systems, like smart healthcare, energy grids and industrial automation, appear, it is easier to see the shortcomings of older methods of detection. Monitoring failures usually happen in dynamic, high dimensional situations, especially when data is incomplete, messy or always evolving. Such limits point out the requirement for adaptive, intelligent systems that always improve and think. LLMs are now capable of significantly changing how context is understood and semantic inference is done across all types of data. This proposal suggests using an LLM supported contextual reasoning method along with XAI agents to improve how anomalies are found in significant IoT environments. To discover hidden patterns and notice inconsistencies in data streams, it uses attention methods, avoids dealing with details from every time step and uses memory buffers with meaning. Because no code AI stresses transparency and interpretability, people can check and accept the AI's decisions, helping ensure AI follows company policies. The two architectures are put together in a test that compares the results of the traditional model with those of the suggested LLM enhanced model. Important measures to check are the accuracy of detection, how much inaccurate information is included in the results, how clearly the findings can be read and how fast the system responds under different test situations. The metaheuristic is tested in simulations of real world smart grid and healthcare contexts to check its adaptability and reliability. From the study, we see that the new approach performs much better than most existing models in both accuracy and interpretation, so it could be a good fit for future anomaly detection tasks in IoT"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"22 pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03859","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T03:00:12Z","registered":"2025-10-07T03:00:13Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T03:00:13Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03847","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03847","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03847","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Raghav","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Raghav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Manan","familyName":"Mehta","name":"Mehta, Manan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Small Language Models for Agentic Systems: A Survey of Architectures, Capabilities, and Deployment Trade offs"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T15:48:04Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:37:43Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Small language models (SLMs; 1-12B params, sometimes up to 20B) are sufficient and often superior for agentic workloads where the objective is schema- and API-constrained accuracy rather than open-ended generation. We synthesize recent evidence across open and proprietary SLMs (Phi-4-Mini, Qwen-2.5-7B, Gemma-2-9B, Llama-3.2-1B/3B, Ministral-3B/8B, Apple on-device 3B, DeepSeek-R1-Distill) and connect it to modern evaluations (BFCL v3/v4, StableToolBench) and serving stacks (vLLM, SGLang, TensorRT-LLM) paired with guided decoding libraries (XGrammar, Outlines). We formalize SLM-default, LLM-fallback systems with uncertainty-aware routing and verifier cascades, and propose engineering metrics that reflect real production goals: cost per successful task (CPS), schema validity rate, executable call rate, p50/p95 latency, and energy per request. Guided decoding, strict JSON Schema outputs, and validator-first tool execution close much of the capability gap with larger models and often let SLMs match or surpass LLMs on tool use, function calling, and RAG at 10x-100x lower token cost with materially better latency and energy. We provide design patterns for agent stacks that prioritize SLMs: schema-first prompting, type-safe function registries, confidence scoring with verifier rollups, and lightweight adaptation via LoRA/QLoRA. We also delineate limits where fallback remains valuable (open-domain reasoning and some long-horizon planning). The result is a practical blueprint for building fast, inexpensive, and reliable agents that default to SLMs while preserving headroom with targeted LLM assistance.\n Keywords: small language models, agents, function calling, structured outputs, JSON Schema, guided decoding, LoRA/QLoRA, routing, energy efficiency, edge inference"},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"9 Pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03847","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:59:54Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:59:54Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:59:54Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03840","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03840","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03840","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pranav","familyName":"Sharma","name":"Sharma, Pranav","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shivank","familyName":"Garg","name":"Garg, Shivank","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Durga","familyName":"Toshniwal","name":"Toshniwal, Durga","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mirage: Unveiling Hidden Artifacts in Synthetic Images with Large Vision-Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T15:38:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:37:25Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Recent advances in image generation models have led to models that produce synthetic images that are increasingly difficult for standard AI detectors to identify, even though they often remain distinguishable by humans. To identify this discrepancy, we introduce \\textbf{Mirage}, a curated dataset comprising a diverse range of AI-generated images exhibiting visible artifacts, where current state-of-the-art detection methods largely fail. Furthermore, we investigate whether Large Vision-Language Models (LVLMs), which are increasingly employed as substitutes for human judgment in various tasks, can be leveraged for explainable AI image detection. Our experiments on both Mirage and existing benchmark datasets demonstrate that while LVLMs are highly effective at detecting AI-generated images with visible artifacts, their performance declines when confronted with images lacking such cues."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"ACM MM'25, MALLM Workshop"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03840","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:59:43Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:59:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:59:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03833","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03833","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03833","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shuoyan","familyName":"Wei","name":"Wei, Shuoyan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Feng","familyName":"Li","name":"Li, Feng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Shengeng","familyName":"Tang","name":"Tang, Shengeng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Runmin","familyName":"Cong","name":"Cong, Runmin","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yao","familyName":"Zhao","name":"Zhao, Yao","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Meng","familyName":"Wang","name":"Wang, Meng","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Huihui","familyName":"Bai","name":"Bai, Huihui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Towards Robust and Generalizable Continuous Space-Time Video Super-Resolution with Events"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Image and Video Processing (eess.IV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computer Vision and Pattern Recognition (cs.CV)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Multimedia (cs.MM)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T15:23:07Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:36:59Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsUri":"http://arxiv.org/licenses/nonexclusive-distrib/1.0/","rights":"arXiv.org perpetual, non-exclusive license"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Continuous space-time video super-resolution (C-STVSR) has garnered increasing interest for its capability to reconstruct high-resolution and high-frame-rate videos at arbitrary spatial and temporal scales. However, prevailing methods often generalize poorly, producing unsatisfactory results when applied to out-of-distribution (OOD) scales. To overcome this limitation, we present EvEnhancer, a novel approach that marries the unique properties of high temporal resolution and high dynamic range encapsulated in event streams to achieve robust and generalizable C-STVSR. Our approach incorporates event-adapted synthesis that capitalizes on the spatiotemporal correlations between frames and events to capture long-term motion trajectories, enabling adaptive interpolation and fusion across space and time. This is then coupled with a local implicit video transformer that integrates local implicit video neural function with cross-scale spatiotemporal attention to learn continuous video representations and generate plausible videos at arbitrary resolutions and frame rates. We further develop EvEnhancerPlus, which builds a controllable switching mechanism that dynamically determines the reconstruction difficulty for each spatiotemporal pixel based on local event statistics. This allows the model to adaptively route reconstruction along the most suitable pathways at a fine-grained pixel level, substantially reducing computational overhead while maintaining excellent performance. Furthermore, we devise a cross-derivative training strategy that stabilizes the convergence of such a multi-pathway framework through staged cross-optimization. Extensive experiments demonstrate that our method achieves state-of-the-art performance on both synthetic and real-world datasets, while maintaining superior generalizability at OOD scales. The code is available at https://github.com/W-Shuoyan/EvEnhancerPlus."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"17 pages, 12 figures, 14 tables. Under review"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03833","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:59:33Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:59:34Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:59:34Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03829","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03829","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03829","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"André","familyName":"Coelho","name":"Coelho, André","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Pedro","familyName":"Ribeiro","name":"Ribeiro, Pedro","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Helder","familyName":"Fontes","name":"Fontes, Helder","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Rui","familyName":"Campos","name":"Campos, Rui","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A4FN: an Agentic AI Architecture for Autonomous Flying Networks"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Networking and Internet Architecture (cs.NI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T15:02:03Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:36:37Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-nd/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution Non Commercial No Derivatives 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-nc-nd-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This position paper presents A4FN, an Agentic Artificial Intelligence (AI) architecture for intent-driven automation in Flying Networks (FNs) using Unmanned Aerial Vehicles (UAVs) as access nodes. A4FN leverages Generative AI and Large Language Models (LLMs) to enable real-time, context-aware network control via a distributed agentic system. It comprises two components: the Perception Agent (PA), which semantically interprets multimodal input -- including imagery, audio, and telemetry data -- from UAV-mounted sensors to derive Service Level Specifications (SLSs); and the Decision-and-Action Agent (DAA), which reconfigures the network based on inferred intents. A4FN embodies key properties of Agentic AI, including autonomy, goal-driven reasoning, and continuous perception-action cycles. Designed for mission-critical, infrastructure-limited scenarios such as disaster response, it supports adaptive reconfiguration, dynamic resource management, and interoperability with emerging wireless technologies. The paper details the A4FN architecture, its core innovations, and open research challenges in multi-agent coordination and Agentic AI integration in next-generation FNs."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"This paper has been accepted for presentation in the Auto ML for Zero-Touch Network Management Workshop (WS04-01) at the IEEE International Symposium on Personal, Indoor and Mobile Radio Communications (PIMRC) 2025"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03829","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:59:27Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:59:27Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:59:27Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03815","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03815","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03815","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Yue","familyName":"wu","name":"wu, Yue","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"A Trustworthy Industrial Fault Diagnosis Architecture Integrating Probabilistic Models and Large Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Systems and Control (eess.SY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Machine Learning (cs.LG)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Signal Processing (eess.SP)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Electrical engineering, electronic engineering, information engineering","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T14:11:13Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:35:27Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"There are limitations of traditional methods and deep learning methods in terms of interpretability, generalization, and quantification of uncertainty in industrial fault diagnosis, and there are core problems of insufficient credibility in industrial fault diagnosis. The architecture performs preliminary analysis through a Bayesian network-based diagnostic engine and features an LLM-driven cognitive quorum module with multimodal input capabilities. The module conducts expert-level arbitration of initial diagnoses by analyzing structured features and diagnostic charts, prioritizing final decisions after conflicts are identified. To ensure the reliability of the system output, the architecture integrates a confidence calibration module based on temperature calibration and a risk assessment module, which objectively quantifies the reliability of the system using metrics such as expected calibration error (ECE). Experimental results on a dataset containing multiple fault types showed that the proposed framework improved diagnostic accuracy by more than 28 percentage points compared to the baseline model, while the calibrated ECE was reduced by more than 75%. Case studies have confirmed that HCAA effectively corrects misjudgments caused by complex feature patterns or knowledge gaps in traditional models, providing novel and practical engineering solutions for building high-trust, explainable AI diagnostic systems for industrial applications."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"1tables,6 figs,11pages"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03815","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:59:06Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:59:07Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:59:07Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03808","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03808","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03808","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mehedi Hasan","familyName":"Emon","name":"Emon, Mehedi Hasan","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Annotate Rhetorical Relations with INCEpTION: A Comparison with Automatic Approaches"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T13:33:42Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:34:34Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This research explores the annotation of rhetorical relations in discourse using the INCEpTION tool and compares manual annotation with automatic approaches based on large language models. The study focuses on sports reports (specifically cricket news) and evaluates the performance of BERT, DistilBERT, and Logistic Regression models in classifying rhetorical relations such as elaboration, contrast, background, and cause-effect. The results show that DistilBERT achieved the highest accuracy, highlighting its potential for efficient discourse relation prediction. This work contributes to the growing intersection of discourse parsing and transformer-based NLP. (This paper was conducted as part of an academic requirement under the supervision of Prof. Dr. Ralf Klabunde, Linguistic Data Science Lab, Ruhr University Bochum.) Keywords: Rhetorical Structure Theory, INCEpTION, BERT, DistilBERT, Discourse Parsing, NLP."}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03808","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:58:56Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:58:57Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:58:57Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03799","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03799","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03799","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Hadi","familyName":"Asghari","name":"Asghari, Hadi","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Sami","familyName":"Nenno","name":"Nenno, Sami","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Mechanistic Interpretability of Socio-Political Frames in Language Models"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Artificial Intelligence (cs.AI)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computers and Society (cs.CY)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T12:39:39Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:33:35Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"This paper explores the ability of large language models to generate and recognize deep cognitive frames, particularly in socio-political contexts. We demonstrate that LLMs are highly fluent in generating texts that evoke specific frames and can recognize these frames in zero-shot settings. Inspired by mechanistic interpretability research, we investigate the location of the `strict father' and `nurturing parent' frames within the model's hidden representation, identifying singular dimensions that correlate strongly with their presence. Our findings contribute to understanding how LLMs capture and express meaningful human concepts."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"Peer-reviewed and presented at Advances in Interpretable Machine Learning and Artificial Intelligence (AIMLAI) Workshop at ECML/PKDD 2024"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03799","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:58:43Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:58:44Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:58:44Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}},{"id":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03795","type":"dois","attributes":{"doi":"10.48550/arxiv.2510.03795","identifiers":[{"identifier":"2510.03795","identifierType":"arXiv"}],"creators":[{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Simon","familyName":"Lupart","name":"Lupart, Simon","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Daniël","familyName":"van Dijk","name":"van Dijk, Daniël","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Eric","familyName":"Langezaal","name":"Langezaal, Eric","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Ian","familyName":"van Dort","name":"van Dort, Ian","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]},{"nameType":"Personal","givenName":"Mohammad","familyName":"Aliannejadi","name":"Aliannejadi, Mohammad","affiliation":[],"nameIdentifiers":[]}],"titles":[{"title":"Investigating LLM Variability in Personalized Conversational Information Retrieval"}],"publisher":"arXiv","container":{},"publicationYear":2025,"subjects":[{"subject":"Information Retrieval (cs.IR)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"Computation and Language (cs.CL)","lang":"en","subjectScheme":"arXiv"},{"subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"},{"schemeUri":"http://www.oecd.org/science/inno/38235147.pdf","subject":"FOS: Computer and information sciences","subjectScheme":"Fields of Science and Technology (FOS)"}],"contributors":[],"dates":[{"date":"2025-10-04T12:13:19Z","dateType":"Submitted","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10-07T00:33:21Z","dateType":"Updated","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025-10","dateType":"Available","dateInformation":"v1"},{"date":"2025","dateType":"Issued"}],"language":null,"types":{"schemaOrg":"CreativeWork","resourceTypeGeneral":"Preprint","citeproc":"article","bibtex":"misc","ris":"GEN","resourceType":"Article"},"relatedIdentifiers":[],"relatedItems":[],"sizes":[],"formats":[],"version":"1","rightsList":[{"rightsIdentifierScheme":"SPDX","rightsUri":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/legalcode","schemeUri":"https://spdx.org/licenses/","rights":"Creative Commons Attribution 4.0 International","rightsIdentifier":"cc-by-4.0"}],"descriptions":[{"descriptionType":"Abstract","description":"Personalized Conversational Information Retrieval (CIR) has seen rapid progress in recent years, driven by the development of Large Language Models (LLMs). Personalized CIR aims to enhance document retrieval by leveraging user-specific information, such as preferences, knowledge, or constraints, to tailor responses to individual needs. A key resource for this task is the TREC iKAT 2023 dataset, designed to evaluate personalization in CIR pipelines. Building on this resource, Mo et al. explored several strategies for incorporating Personal Textual Knowledge Bases (PTKB) into LLM-based query reformulation. Their findings suggested that personalization from PTKBs could be detrimental and that human annotations were often noisy. However, these conclusions were based on single-run experiments using the GPT-3.5 Turbo model, raising concerns about output variability and repeatability. In this reproducibility study, we rigorously reproduce and extend their work, focusing on LLM output variability and model generalization. We apply the original methods to the new TREC iKAT 2024 dataset and evaluate a diverse range of models, including Llama (1B-70B), Qwen-7B, GPT-4o-mini. Our results show that human-selected PTKBs consistently enhance retrieval performance, while LLM-based selection methods do not reliably outperform manual choices. We further compare variance across datasets and observe higher variability on iKAT than on CAsT, highlighting the challenges of evaluating personalized CIR. Notably, recall-oriented metrics exhibit lower variance than precision-oriented ones, a critical insight for first-stage retrievers. Finally, we underscore the need for multi-run evaluations and variance reporting when assessing LLM-based CIR systems. By broadening evaluation across models, datasets, and metrics, our study contributes to more robust and generalizable practices for personalized CIR."},{"descriptionType":"Other","description":"11 pages, 5 figures, SIGIR-AP'25 Proceedings of the 2025 Annual International ACM SIGIR Conference on Research and Development in Information Retrieval in the Asia Pacific Region (SIGIR-AP 2025), December 7--10, 2025, Xi'an, China"}],"geoLocations":[],"fundingReferences":[],"url":"https://arxiv.org/abs/2510.03795","contentUrl":null,"metadataVersion":0,"schemaVersion":"http://datacite.org/schema/kernel-4","source":"mds","isActive":true,"state":"findable","reason":null,"viewCount":0,"downloadCount":0,"referenceCount":0,"citationCount":0,"partCount":0,"partOfCount":0,"versionCount":0,"versionOfCount":0,"created":"2025-10-07T02:58:37Z","registered":"2025-10-07T02:58:38Z","published":null,"updated":"2025-10-07T02:58:38Z"},"relationships":{"client":{"data":{"id":"arxiv.content","type":"clients"}}}}],"meta":{"total":372,"totalPages":4,"page":3},"links":{"self":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-06T16%3A00%3A00Z%20TO%202025-10-07T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D%20AND%20(%22language%20model%22%20OR%20%22language%20models%22%20OR%20LLM%20OR%20LLMs%20OR%20transformer%20OR%20transformers%20OR%20%22mixture%20of%20experts%22)\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=3","next":"https://api.datacite.org/dois?client-id=arxiv.content\u0026page%5Bnumber%5D=4\u0026page%5Bsize%5D=100\u0026query=registered%3A%5B2025-10-06T16%3A00%3A00Z+TO+2025-10-07T15%3A59%3A59Z%5D+AND+%28%22language+model%22+OR+%22language+models%22+OR+LLM+OR+LLMs+OR+transformer+OR+transformers+OR+%22mixture+of+experts%22%29"}}